T1  - RETROGRADE INTUBATION IN ADVANCED NASOPHARYNGEAL CARCINOMA WITH AIRWAY OBSTRUCTION
T3  - Official Publications of the American College of Chest Physicians. Chicago United States.
A1  - Wagle L.
A1  - Regmi D.
A1  - Abdulmahdi M.
A1  - Greco B.D.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - SESSION TITLE: Procedure Related Complications in Critical Care SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/20/2025 12:30 pm - 01:15 pm INTRODUCTION: Retrograde intubation is a surgical airway management technique involving translaryngeal guidance for orotracheal or nasotracheal intubation (1). This approach is often critical when conventional intubation methods are ineffective, such as in cases of airway obstruction due to large head and neck tumors. CASE PRESENTATION: A 36-year-old male with stage IVA Epstein-Barr virus-positive nasopharyngeal carcinoma, undergoing chemotherapy with cisplatin and gemcitabine, presented with dyspnea, dysphagia, and difficulty managing secretions. His symptoms intensified after his recent chemotherapy cycle. On arrival, he was in significant respiratory distress with a respiratory rate of 30 breaths per minute and oxygen saturation of 98%. Examination showed a muffled voice, drooling, accessory muscle use, and stridor. A CT scan revealed a large, necrotic mass causing near-total obstruction of the posterior pharynx (Figure 1 and 2). Laboratory results showed neutrophil-predominant leukocytosis and respiratory acidosis. While the tumor was the primary cause of airway obstruction, other complications like infection were considered.Due to the failure of video laryngoscopy and tumor obstruction, retrograde intubation was performed. After preoxygenation and light sedation with ketamine, local anesthesia was applied at the cricothyroid membrane, and a wire was retrogradely passed through the oral cavity. A 6.0 endotracheal tube was placed and confirmed with end-tidal CO2 and bronchoscopy. After successful intubation, a bronchoscopy-guided tracheostomy was performed, and the endotracheal tube was removed. The patient was treated with steroids and palliative radiation therapy. He was discharged after clinical stabilization with a trach collar with close outpatient follow-up. DISCUSSION(S): Retrograde endotracheal intubation (REI), introduced in 1960, is a technique used when conventional intubation fails due to anatomical or obstructive issues. The method involves puncturing the cricothyroid membrane, inserting a guidewire, and advancing an endotracheal tube over it (2, 3). REI has become a crucial tool for managing airways in head and neck cancer patients, where traditional techniques like fiberoptic intubation may fail due to tumor-induced distortion (4). Unlike tracheostomy, which carries higher morbidity risks, REI is minimally invasive with a low complication profile (2, 3).While fiberoptic intubation is considered the gold standard, it is not always feasible in emergency settings due to tumor invasion, bleeding, or secretions, which obscure the view (4). In such cases, REI provides a reliable alternative with fewer risks, including sore throat or minor bleeding, which are generally self-limiting (3). Studies show that REI is an effective and safe technique, especially in resource-limited environments or when fiberoptic equipment is unavailable (4). Its ability to bypass obstructed airways makes it an essential skill for emergency and critical care providers (2,3). CONCLUSION(S): Retrograde intubation is a highly effective and minimally invasive technique for managing airway obstruction in patients with advanced nasopharyngeal carcinoma. This case report demonstrates the utility of retrograde intubation when conventional methods fail, highlighting its low complication rate and high success in securing the airway. It serves as a valuable tool in emergency airway management, particularly in patients with complex head and neck malignancies. Retrograde intubation should be considered a critical skill for clinicians involved in airway management, especially in high-risk and resource-limited settings. REFERENCE #1: 1. Burbulys D, Kiai K. Retrograde Intubation. Emerg Med Clin North Am. 2008 Nov;26(4):1029-41. REFERENCE #2: 2. Marchello, Vincenzo MDa; Corso, Ruggero M. MDb; Piraccini, Emanuele MDb; Del Gaudio, Alfredo MDa; Mincolelli, Giuseppe MDa; Cattano, Davide MD, PhDc. The retrograde tracheal intubation technique for difficult airway management: old but still working. Journal of Head & Neck Anesthesia 3(2):p e16, May 2019.  DOI: 10.1097/HN9.0000000000000016 REFERENCE #3: 3. Tiwari T, Walian A, Singh VK, Singh V, Chakraborty S, Rawat A. Evaluation of retrograde intubation with different doses of dexmedetomidine infusion: A randomised controlled trial. J Oral Biol Craniofac Res. 2020 Jul-Sep;10(3):304-309. doi: 10.1016/j.jobcr.2020.06.002. Epub 2020 Jun 18. PMID: 32637307; PMCID: PMC7327427.4. Weksler N, Klein M, Weksler D, Sidelnick C, Chorni I, Rozentsveig V, Brill S, Gurman GM, Ovadia L. Retrograde tracheal intubation: beyond fibreoptic endotracheal intubation. Acta Anaesthesiol Scand. 2004 Apr;48(4):412-6. doi: 10.1111/j.0001-5172.2004.00347.x. PMID: 15025601. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Mustafa Abdulmahdi No relevant relationships by Brian Del Greco No relevant relationships by Dhiraj Regmi No relevant relationships by Laxman WagleCopyright © 2025 American College of Chest Physicians
