T1  - ETOMIDATE AS AN ALTERNATIVE TO ANTIANDROGENIC THERAPY IN A CASE OF HYPERCORTISOLISM
T3  - Official Publications of the American College of Chest Physicians. Chicago United States.
A1  - Katari V.
A1  - Lopez A.
A1  - Guck D.G.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Report Posters (V) SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/21/2025 10:20 am - 11:05 am INTRODUCTION: Hypercortisolism leads to a constellation of signs, symptoms, and impaired immunity that can be life threatening, especially for critically ill patients. A number of oral therapies are available to reduce serum cortisol including Ketoconazole, Metyrapone, and Mitotane; however, data regarding intravenous alternatives is limited.In recent years, Etomidate has been studied as an intravenous alternative for antiandrogenic therapy. Etomidate is a short acting analgesic that is known to reduce steroidogenesis by inhibiting 11-beta-hydroxylase, an enzyme involved in cortisol synthesis. Here we present a case of a young female with metastatic adrenocortical carcinoma who was admitted to the ICU with respiratory failure requiring intubation and was started on an Etomidate drip for cortisol reduction. CASE PRESENTATION: A 26-year-old female with active metastatic adrenocortical carcinoma presented with acute respiratory failure secondary to aspiration requiring intubation. Prior to this hospitalization, she had completed 2 rounds of chemotherapy, and despite this had progression of her malignancy. She was on Mitotane and Metyrapone for hypercortisolism. Following intubation, her oral therapies were not able to be crushed and she went without antiadrenergic therapy for 72 hours, with rapid worsening of her cortisol levels to >200. Following a number of multidisciplinary discussions between pharmacy, endocrine, oncology, and the primary ICU team, the decision was made to trial an Etomidate drip in efforts to reduce total serum cortisol levels.The Etomidate drip was initiated on day 4 of ICU admission, starting with a 5mg IV bolus followed by continuous drip at a rate of 0.02mg/kg/hr. At time of initiation, serum cortisol was 213. Serum cortisol levels were monitored every 6 hours for the first 24 hours, then checked daily between 7-9am. Etomidate was titrated to achieve serum cortisol<25. Initially there was rise in serum cortisol to 249, requiring up titration of the drip, at highest rate of 0.14 mg/kg/hr. She was eventually maintained on a rate of 0.12mg/kg/hr to maintain serum cortisol<25, until family decided to pursue comfort care measures and the drip was discontinued. DISCUSSION(S): Adequate control of hypercortisolism can be quite challenging given the minimal availability of anti-adrenergic drugs, and this becomes more difficult in patients without PO access. For our patient, we followed the proposed guideline for etomidate infusion published in Carroll et al1. We have demonstrated the success we had with IV etomidate in not only lowering the cortisol levels, but also stabilizing hemodynamics to allow management of her other acute comorbid conditions. A multidisciplinary team involving endocrinology, oncology, pharmacy, and the intensive care team was imperative in ensuring our patient's safety. There have been a handful of other case reports outlining the use of etomidate for cortisol reduction, but literature still remains quite limited. CONCLUSION(S): Without enteral access, conventional oral therapies for hypercortisolism are unavailable. Etomidate should be considered in intubated patients requiring rapid serum cortisol reduction to prevent lapses in antiandrogenic therapy. REFERENCE #1: Carroll, T. B., Peppard, W. J., Herrmann, D. J., Javorsky, B. R., Wang, T. S., Patel, H., Zarnecki, K., & Findling, J. W. (2018). Continuous Etomidate Infusion for the Management of Severe Cushing Syndrome: Validation of a Standard Protocol. Journal of the Endocrine Society, 3(1), 1-12. https://doi.org/10.1210/js.2018-00269 REFERENCE #2: Wagner, R. L., White, P. F., Kan, P. B., Rosenthal, M. H., & Feldman, D. (1984). Inhibition of adrenal steroidogenesis by the anesthetic etomidate. The New England journal of medicine, 310(22), 1415-1421. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJM198405313102202 REFERENCE #3: N/a DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Daniel Guck No relevant relationships by Varsha Katari No relevant relationships by Alexandra LopezCopyright © 2025 American College of Chest Physicians
