T1  - Opioid-free Sedation in Patients with Severe Traumatic Brain Injury. A Feasibility Study
T3  - CRITICARE 2025. Cochin India.
A1  - Arora S.
A1  - Karki Y.
A1  - Jain K.
A1  - Bhatia N.
A1  - Hazarika A.
A1  - Aggarwal A.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Introduction: Traumatic brain injury (TBI) is a leading cause of death and hospitalisations worldwide, marked by primary and secondaryinjuries. Sedation plays a crucial role in the comprehensive management of patients with severe TBI.1 It serves as a preventive treatment toavoid secondary brain insults and facilitates the implementation of other interventions like mechanical ventilation. Propofol and opioidsare one of the most commonly used drugs for sedation in TBI. However, the use of opioids is associated with an array of side effects.2Current literature suggest that ketamine & dexmedetomidine can be used in TBI patients.3,4 Objective: This study evaluates the efficacyand safety of an opioid-free sedation regimen using ketamine-dexmedetomidine (Group KD) compared to the standard propofol-fentanylcombination (Group PO) in patients with severe TBI admitted to trauma intensive care unit. The study aims to identify a sedation strategythat ensures effective sedation and analgesia while reducing the opioid-related side effect burden. The primary objective was to find outthe "percentage of time spent in goal Richmond Agitation Sedation (RASS) score, i.e., RASS score of -3 to -4" in two groups. Secondaryobjectives were to note the incidence of hemodynamic alterations, side effects of drugs, decrease in serum cortisol level at 48 hours &FOUR score at ICU discharge. Methodology: This study is a prospective, randomised trial conducted in the trauma ICU of Level 1 TraumaCentre. Thirty patients with severe traumatic brain injury requiring intubation and mechanical ventilation were enrolled in the study, 15in each group. Ketamine and Dexmedetomidine were given as sedation in Group KD, and Propofol and fentanyl in Group PO. Sedationwas initiated and titrated to achieve a target RASS score of -3 to -4, checked every 15 minutes initially and hourly after that for 48 hours.Hemodynamic alterations, incidence of agitation (RASS>+1), VAP, withdrawal symptoms, decrease in serum cortisol level at the end of48 hrs, FOUR score and any neuro worsening were noted. Result(s): Thirty patients were enrolled, with comparable demographics in bothgroups. Patients in both groups maintained target RASS scores for similar durations (94.4% in Group KD vs. 98% in Group PO, p = 0.279).Serum cortisol reduction was significantly greater in Group KD (38% vs. 13.8%, p = 0.0002). The incidence of bradycardia was higher ingroup KD (p=0.002), whereas more patients in Group PO had withdrawal symptoms (p = 0.0002) and constipation (p = 0.0078). Agitationand VAP rates were similar. Three patients in group KD and one patient in group PO got extubated with FOUR score of 16 on dischargefrom the ICU. The four score on discharge from the ICU ranged from 8 to 16 with similar frequency across different scores between thegroups (all scores had p-value >0.05). There was one case of neuro worsening in group KD. Conclusion(s): Ketamine-Dexmedetomidinesedation for conservative management of severe TBI patients is as effective as the more practiced propofol-fentanyl sedation whileavoiding the significant side effects of opioid use. This study highlights non-opioid drug combinations as a promising alternative forregular analgosedative in the management of TBI patients.
