T1  - Sedation practices in out-of-hospital cardiac arrest: a Danish prospective cohort study
T3  - Acute Cardiovascular Care Congress 2025. Florence Italy.
A1  - Charabi S.S.
A1  - Alaoui-Ismaili Y.A.I.
A1  - Meyer M.M.
A1  - Eifer-Moeller J.E.M.
A1  - Obling L.O.
A1  - Beske R.B.
A1  - Hassager C.H.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Background: Sedation is commonly used following the intubation of resuscitated out-of-hospital cardiac arrest (OHCA). The sedatives employed have potent yet varying hemodynamic effects, which can complicate management during the fragile post-resuscitation phase. Purpose(s): To evaluate the choice and clinical impact of sedatives in intubated OHCA patients, aiming to identify patterns to inform management strategies and standardize sedation protocols in prehospital care. Method(s): In this prospective cohort study, we analyzed prehospital medical records of 67 patients who experienced OHCA across two Danish regions from 2023 to 2024. The cohort was identified from an ongoing OHCA trial, including adult patients (>=18 years) with OHCA of presumed cardiac origin who achieved sustained return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC) for more than 20 minutes. Intubated patients were identified and categorized as non-sedated or sedated with either propofol, ketamine, or benzodiazepines. We examined differences in demographics, OHCA presentation and hemodynamic, metabolic, and survival status between groups. Result(s): Of 67 patients, 58 were intubated in the prehospital setting. Of these, 44/58 (76%) were sedated while 14/58 (24 %) were not sedated. Propofol was used in 32/44 (73%), ketamine in 3/44 (7%), and benzodiazepines in 3/44 (7%), while the sedative agent was unknown in remaining 6 cases (figure 1). Ketamine was only administered to patients with suspected traumatic head injury. First recorded post-ROSC systolic blood pressures were similar between the sedated and non-sedated groups. Vasopressor use differed markedly: 25 % of sedated patients (11/44) vs. 7% of the non-sedated patients (1/14). Non-sedated patients had longer time to ROSC compared to sedated patients (median 30 minutes (interquartile range (IQR) 18-45) vs. 15 minutes (IQR 12-22), p=0.001), higher arterial lactate at hospital admission (median 9.1 mmol/L (IQR 2.4-7.5) vs. 3.6 (IQR 2.0-5.6) mmol/L, p<0.001) and higher mortality at discharge: 7/14 (50%) vs. 10/44 (23%) (figure 2). Conclusion(s): Across two Danish regions, propofol was the preferred sedative in intubation following out-of-hospital cardiac arrest. The use of vasopressors was markedly higher in sedated- compared to non-sedated patients. Sedated patients had shorter time to ROSC, lower arterial lactate at hospital admission and lower mortality at discharge compared to non-sedated patients, suggesting that sedation was more likely in patients with a better immediate prognosis.
