T1  - Serotonin Surge: Unraveling the Puzzle of Serotonin Syndrome in the ICU
T3  - International Anesthesia Research Society Annual Meeting, IARS 2025. Honolulu, HI United States.
A1  - Glasgow H.
A1  - Gorman B.
A1  - Krogman W.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Introduction: Serotonin syndrome (SS) is a rare, life-threatening condition that can present when multiple serotonergic medications are jointly administered. In the perioperative setting, SS can present similarly to several conditions, such as malignant hyperthermia (MH) and neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS).1,2 We report the case of a patient on paroxetine at home who developed SS shortly after initiation of a fentanyl drip in the ICU shortly after a left atrial appendage closure. Method(s): A 74-year-old male with a history of hypertension, paroxysmal atrial fibrillation on anticoagulation, anxiety, and recent gastrointestinal bleed presented for elective left atrial appendage closure with a WATCHMAN implant device. The patient received fentanyl intraoperatively and anesthesia was maintained with isoflurane. The procedure was complicated by a left atrial appendage perforation and he was brought back to the ICU intubated and sedated on propofol. A fentanyl drip was added for sedation as the propofol was weaned. On POD 1, he developed erythematous face and neck and increased abdominal tone. He was treated with IV diphenhydramine and steroids for possible allergic reaction to contrast. His abdominal tone and erythema worsened through the day so he was given midazolam 6 h after receiving diphenhydramine and steroids, which decreased abdominal tone for 2 h. At that time, his muscle tone worsened and included bilateral upper and lower extremities (BLE). The next day, anesthesia was consulted for a potential anesthesia-related reaction. At rest, his extremities appeared rigid, worse in BLE, but the patient could overcome rigidity to follow commands. The patient's abdomen was rigid with hypoactive bowel sounds. Clonus was elicited in BLE and in the right thumb. Deep tendon reflexes were difficult to appreciate due to his rigidity. On exam, he was tachycardic and on a low dose pressor. He had an elevated lactic acid that was down trending. Potassium and PaCO2 levels remained stable, and his highest skin temperature was 37.5degreeC. Given the down-trending lactate, stable PaCO2, and potassium, MH was ruled out. Upon review of his home medications, there were no potential triggers for NMS, but the patient was on paroxetine. Using the Hunter Serotonin Toxicity Criteria (HSTC), anesthesia determined that this patient met the criteria of being on a serotonergic agent, with questionable spontaneous clonus, inducible clonus with hypertonia, with some agitation that required sedation. The team recommended a trial of cyproheptadine and discontinuing the fentanyl drip. The patient received his first dose within the hour and was placed on a midazolam drip for agitation. The patient was extubated on POD 4 and discharged on POD 8. Result(s): Not applicable - medically challenging case report Conclusion(s): MH and NMS are two conditions that have similar, life-threatening presentations to SS.1,2 Overlapping signs and symptoms include elevated temperature and muscle rigidity.1 Methylene blue3, meperidine4, and fentanyl5 have all been shown to be triggers for SS in patients already taking a serotonergic agent, especially in the perioperative setting. Ondansetron and lidocaine have also been implicated in the development of SS perioperatively.1 Diagnosis of SS is usually based on one of two different clinical criteria: the Sternbach criteria or the HSTC. For treatment, benzodiazepines can be used for symptomatic relief in SS1,4, but cyproheptadine is regarded as first line treatment for SS.1 The susceptibility of some people over others for the development of SS remains unclear. It may be that the incidence of SS is underreported because it is underdiagnosed1, which could be due to the HSTC being too specific and the range of symptom severity.6 Certain drug combinations are thought of as more likely to result in moderate to severe SS, including the combination of selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors and opiates.6 Serotonin syndrome is considered a rare diagnosis but may be more common than previously believed. Given the growing number of patients on a serotonergic agent5 and the increasing number of surgeries occurring each year, it is important for anesthesiologists to recognize patients at risk in the perioperative period, including post operatively in an ICU setting, and to differentiate potential serotonin syndrome from other lifethreatening diagnoses.
