T1  - 418 Intravenous Push Ketamine for Post-Intubation Sedation in the Prehospital Setting
T3  - ACEP Research Forum 2025. Salt Palace Convention Center, Salt Lake City United States.
A1  - Grillo M.
A1  - Procopio G.
A1  - Wolff A.
A1  - Rosario A.
A1  - Gelman S.
A1  - Nyirenda T.
A1  - Patterson S.
A1  - Sugalski G.
A1  - Berns A.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Study Objective: The objective of this study was to evaluate cardiovascular outcomes in patients who received ketamine versus alternative sedative agents for post-intubation sedation in the prehospital setting. Method(s): This was a single-center, retrospective review at an academic medical center. Records of patients brought to the adult emergency department at Hackensack University Medical Center and intubated by emergency medical services with post-intubation sedation initiated prior to hospital arrival between October 2017 and October 2023 were reviewed. The primary outcome was a composite endpoint of cardiovascular adverse effects, defined as new electrocardiogram abnormalities, cardiac arrest within 24 hours of arrival, and abnormal troponin levels (> 0.03 ng/mL). Secondary endpoints included electrocardiogram abnormalities, changes in troponin levels, incidence of cardiac arrest in the first 24 hours, Riker Sedation-Agitation score upon arrival, additional sedatives administered in the first 24 hours, changes in hemodynamic stability in the first two hours, emergency department length of stay, time to extubation, and in-hospital mortality. Result(s): A total of 243 patients were included. The median age of patients in the ketamine group was 67 years and the median age of patients in the other sedative group was 63 years. Males represented 61% of the cohort. Out of 134 patients in the other sedative group, 29 also received ketamine as an induction agent (21.6%). Out of 109 patients in the ketamine group, 97 also received ketamine for induction (89%). Overall, patients who received ketamine for post-intubation sedation did not experience significantly more cardiovascular adverse effects (68 of 109, 62.4%) versus patients who received any other agent (83 of 134, 61.9%), P=0.9433. Patients in the ketamine group were more likely to experience cardiac arrest in the first 24 hours (21 of 109, 19.3%) compared to the other sedative group (11 of 134, 8.2%), P=0.0112. Univariate logistic regression analysis was performed on the cohort of 243 patients to investigate any potential risk factors associated with the composite cardiovascular adverse effect or incidence of cardiac arrest. Significant risk factors in cardiovascular outcomes included age >= 66 years versus < 66 years (P=0.0017; OR 2.337; 95% CI 1.374, 3.976) and pertinent cardiac history (P=0.0030; OR 2.255; 95% CI 1.318, 3.860). Significant risk factors in outcomes for cardiac arrest within the first 24 hours included age >= 66 years versus < 66 years (P=0.0007; OR 4.997; 95% CI 1.975, 12.639) and ketamine administration (P=0.0135; OR 2.668; 95% CI 1.224, 5.816). A multivariate logistic regression analysis is underway to build on the significant factors identified in the univariate analysis stage. Conclusion(s): Results show that ketamine, when used for post-intubation sedation in the prehospital setting, may be associated with increased risk of cardiac arrest in the first 24 hours of emergency department arrival. Randomized controlled trials are needed to assess the safety of ketamine used in this setting. No, authors do not have interests to discloseCopyright © 2025
