T1  - 528 Adverse Effects of Ketamine in Acute Trauma Pain in Emergency: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis
T3  - ACEP Research Forum 2025. Salt Palace Convention Center, Salt Lake City United States.
A1  - Geraldini L.
A1  - Xavier F.
A1  - Silveira D.
A1  - Carvalho O.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Study Objectives: This study aimed to evaluate the adverse effects associated with ketamine compared to other drugs when used for analgesia in trauma patients in emergency settings. While ketamine is increasingly used as an alternative or adjunct to opioids due to its analgesic efficacy and opioid-sparing potential, especially in light of opioid-associated risks, such as abuse and dependence, its safety profile remains a concern. Understanding the frequency and severity of ketamine's adverse effects is crucial for optimizing its use in emergency trauma care. Method(s): We searched PubMed, Embase, and Cochrane databases for randomized controlled trials (RCTs) up to 12 December 2024. After removing duplicates using Zotero, two independent reviewers screened titles and abstracts, followed by a full-text assessment for eligibility. Discrepancies between the authors were resolved through discussions. Exclusion criteria were pediatric patients, ICU patients, sedated or intubated patients, non-trauma patients, and non-RCTs. A random-effects model with 95% confidence intervals was used to estimate risk ratios (RR). Heterogeneity was assessed using the I2 statistic and Cochran's Q Test. Meta-analyses and forest plots were generated using Review Manager (RevMan) 5.4. The protocol was registered in PROSPERO (CRD420251009954, on 14 March 2025). PRISMA guidelines were used to ensure transparency and methodological rigor. We segmented the studies into subgroups by route of administration of ketamine: intravenous (IV), nebulized (NBZ), and intranasal (IN). The primary outcomes of this meta-analysis were pain reduction and the need for rescue analgesia, which are reported in a complementary abstract. Here, we present the secondary outcomes, focusing on the adverse effects, including dizziness, nausea/vomiting, neuropsychological symptoms, visual disturbances, hemodynamic instability, cardiac arrest and death. Result(s): A total of 18 RCTs were included with 3,355 patients (1,717 ketamine vs 1,648 control). The mean age was 34,2 years, with 28,78% female patients. Adverse effects were analyzed according to the route of ketamine administration (IV, IN, and NBZ). No cases of cardiac arrest or death were reported. Hemodynamic instability was documented in only two studies, with two occurrences in the ketamine group and one in the control group. Dizziness was the most frequently reported adverse effect, occurring in 217 patients in the ketamine group compared to 148 in the control group. In the IN subgroup, the incidence of dizziness was significantly higher in the ketamine group (RR 1.69; 95% CI 1.05-2.71; p = 0.03; I2 = 44%), while all other results for this subgroup were not statistically significant. In the IV subgroup, ketamine was associated with a significantly higher incidence of neuropsychological symptoms (RR 4.62; 95% CI 1.98-10.79; p = 0.0004; I2 = 43%) and visual disturbances (RR 3.70; 95% CI 1.59-8.59; p = 0.002; I2 = 0%), with no other statistically significant findings. No statistically significant differences were observed in the NBZ subgroup, nor for nausea and vomiting across any administration route. Conclusion(s): Despite the higher incidence of adverse effects, ketamine appears to be a safe option for analgesia in trauma patients in emergency settings, as no severe adverse events, cardiac arrests, or deaths were reported in any of the studies. The most commonly observed adverse effects, including dizziness and neuropsychological symptoms, were generally transient and self-limiting. The low to moderate heterogeneity in the results may be attributed to variations in ketamine dosing across studies. Given these findings, further high-quality RCTs are needed to better characterize ketamine's safety profile and determine strategies to mitigate its adverse effects while maintaining its analgesic benefits. [Formula presented] No, authors do not have interests to discloseCopyright © 2025
