T1  - 85 Efficacy of Ketamine for Acute Trauma Pain in Emergency: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis
T3  - ACEP Research Forum 2025. Salt Palace Convention Center, Salt Lake City United States.
A1  - Geraldini L.
A1  - Carvalho O.
A1  - Xavier F.
A1  - Silveira D.
Y1  - 2025//
N2  - Study Objectives: This study aimed to assess the analgesic efficacy of ketamine compared to other drugs in trauma patients of any causes treated in emergency settings. Pain management is a key priority in the initial assessment of trauma, although it's often undertreated. While opioids remain the standard of care, ketamine, an NMDA receptor antagonist, has emerged as a promising alternative for a multimodal approach, potentially reducing opioid use and its associated risks, such as abuse and dependence. Method(s): We searched PubMed, Embase, and Cochrane databases for randomized controlled trials (RCTs) up to December 12, 2024. After removing duplicates using Zotero, two independent reviewers screened titles and abstracts, followed by a full-text assessment for eligibility. Discrepancies between the authors were resolved through discussions. Exclusion criteria were pediatric patients, ICU patients, sedated or intubated patients, non-trauma patients, and non-RCTs. The random-effects model with 95% confidence intervals was used to estimate mean differences. The I2 statistic and Cochran's Q Test were used to determine heterogeneity. All meta-analyses and forest plots were generated using Review Manager (RevMan) 5.4. The protocol was registered in PROSPERO (CRD420251009954, on March 14, 2025). PRISMA guidelines were used to ensure transparency and methodological rigor. We segmented the studies into subgroups by route of administration of ketamine: intravenous (IV), nebulized (NBZ), and intranasal (IN). Our primary outcomes were pain score reduction post-intervention, following time points (15 minutes and 30 minutes), and the requirement for rescue analgesics. The secondary outcome was collateral effects, which will be discussed in a separate abstract. Result(s): A total of 18 RCTs were included with 3,355 patients (1,717 ketamine vs 1,648 control). The mean age was 34.2 years, with 28.78% female patients. There was a significant mean difference in pain after ketamine IN versus control at 15 minutes (MD -0.31; CI -0.51, -0.10; p=0.003; I2 = 0%) and at 30 minutes (MD -0.49; CI -0.92, -0.05; p=0.03; I2 45%). We also found a significant difference in mean pain after ketamine IV versus control at 15 minutes (MD -1.12; CI -2.06, -0.19; p=0.02; I2 94%). NBZ route (15 minutes, p=0.4 and 30 minutes, p=0.56) and IV route (30 minutes, p=0.1) were not statistically significant. Ketamine did not significantly reduce the need for rescue analgesics via IV (p=0.89) or IN (p=0.10). For the nebulized route (NBZ), the reduction was statistically significant (p=0.001), but this result should be interpreted with caution as it was derived from a single RCT. Conclusion(s): Ketamine significantly reduced pain at 15 and 30 minutes when administered nasally and at 15 minutes administered intravenously. However, the high weight of one study (Bouida 2020) in IN analysis and the high heterogeneity in IV studies (I2 = 94%) suggests prudence in interpretation. While the results support ketamine as a potential opioid-sparing analgesic in the emergency setting, it did not significantly reduce the need for rescue analgesics via IV or IN. The reduction observed in the NBZ requires caution. Further, high-quality RCTs are needed to optimize dosing strategies and evaluate long-term efficacy. [Formula presented] No, authors do not have interests to discloseCopyright © 2025
