T1  - A Child with Sulfite Oxidase Deficiency Coming for Gastrostomy Tube Exchange - an Anesthetic Challenge
T3  - 18th World Congress of Anaesthesiologists. Singapore Singapore.
A1  - Kurian J.
A1  - Karippuram S.P.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Background: We describe the anesthetic challenge in a 5-year-old child diagnosed with sulfite oxidase deficiency presenting to us for gastrostomy tube exchange. There are no similar cases reported with such anesthesia implication and presentation. There are only 55 such cases reported in medical history. Case Description: This was a 5 yrs. old male child with significant facial anomalies with contractures of limbs. He also had history of seizure for 3 years and was on multiple antiepileptics. Room air saturation was 92% and gross tracheal shift to left from chest Xray and he had gross kyphoscoliosis. After preprocedural evaluation and review of laboratory and radiographic investigations and multidisciplinary meeting was held and a perioperative plan for the conduct of procedure and anesthesia was made. The child was admitted in pediatric intensive unit on the previous day. A trial laryngoscopy was done to evaluate the airway in the pediatric unit by the anesthesia team which visualized the vocal cords with 2 pillows under the neck and shoulder. Plan was made for other difficult airway algorithms including video laryngoscope, fiberoptic scope and tracheostomy in case of failed intubation with direct laryngoscopy. Discussion(s): Anesthetic induction was done with propofol and fentanyl after securing the IV access. The child was positioned with 2 pillows under shoulder and neck to give adequate extension of neck. A check laryngoscopy was done which visualized the larynx and after giving muscle relaxant trachea was successfully intubated. The pediatric surgeon used a rigid esophagoscope to mobilize the gastrostomy tube and the tube was externalized with a small incision on abdomen. But it was noticed the ETC02 was gradually increasing, and airway pressure also was increasing. Significant surgical emphysema was also noted over chest. The endotracheal tube was checked for any kink and other causes of hypercapnia were ruled out. It was decided to ventilate the patient in ICU and observe as it was assumed the hypercapnia and surgical emphysema was due to mechanical ventilation. Over the course of ICU stay the child improved significantly in the ventilatory parameters and surgical emphysema also improved drastically. The child was extubated and was discharged on Day 2. Learning Point: 1.This was the first reported case of sulfite oxidase deficiency patient undergoing a procedure with anesthesia.2.Difficult airway algorithm if followed systematically makes the conduct of anesthesia safe and successful.3.Succinylcholine has to be avoided in such cases as it is associated with other muscle dystrophies. (Figure Presented).
