AN  - 646878251
T1  - Comparison of Awake Nasotracheal Intubation Using Flexible Fibre-Optic Bronchoscope Versus C-MAC D-Blade Video-Laryngoscope in Patients Having Anticipated DiJicult Airway Under Topical Local Anaesthesia and Dexmedetomidine Infusion
T3  - 18th World Congress of Anaesthesiologists. Singapore Singapore.
A1  - Kundu A.
A1  - Patel N.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Background and Objectives: Many indirect laryngoscopes have been introduced into clinical practice and compared with the conventional laryngoscope. We generally use flexible fibreoptic bronchoscope (FOB) in anticipated diJicult intubation. With the advent of CMAC D-Blade Video- Laryngoscope (VL), many anaesthetists have been started to use it for managing anticipated diJicult airway cases. Its ease of use comes handy compared to FOB where many providers have limited experience. Thereby, in our study we compared FOB with C-MAC D blade VL under local anaesthesia and dexmedetomedine sedation in anticipated diJicult airway. The objective of this study was to assess the time required for awake nasotracheal intubation using FOB versus C-MAC VL in anticipated diJicult airway. Method(s): Hundered patieints were randomly divided into two (50 each) groups. Flexible FOB was used to intubate awake patients in group FOB. The C-MAC D-blade VL was used to intubate awake patients in group VL. This is a prospective randomized study. The primary objective was to compare the intubation time between the FOB & VL group. The secondary objectives includes intubation score, use of propofol for rescue, anesthetists' satisfaction, patients' satisfaction, adverse events, hemodynamic changes were all noted. The inclusion criteria - age between 18-75 years, mouth opening >1.5cm,ASA Status I-III, elective surgery requiring nasotracheal intubation and exclusion criteria - patient refusal, severe bradycardia (heart rate <50/min), any type of atrioventricular block in electrocardiogram (ECG),heart failure, base of skull fracture, emergency surgery, thrombocytopaenia or coagulopathy. Result(s): The median intubation time was shorter in the VL group compared to FOB group [63.6 (32.4-71.1) s to 136.2 (120.6-144.6) s (p value<.0001)] respectively There was no diJerence in overall success rate of intubation (98% for FOB vs 96% for VL) but there was significant diJerence in 1st attempt success rate. Propofol rescue was required more in VL group. There was no diJerence in satisfaction of anaesthetists' satisfaction but significant diJerence was seen with patient's satisfaction. There was no significant diJerence in adverse events and hemodynamics. Discussion and Conclusion(s): When compared to the VL group, the intubation time was significantly longer in FOB group compared to VL group . There was a significant diJerence in 1st attempt success where FOB had a more successful 1st attempt compared to the VL group (68% versus 32%,p=0.0004). FOB group had lower intubation score compared to VL group. The amount of Propofol used (mg) was significantly higher in VL group compared to FOB group. The anesthetists' satisfaction were similar but Patients' satisfaction was significantly higher in the FOB group than the VL group. There was no significant diJerence in the number of adverse events and hemodynamic changes between the groups. We conclude that the intubation time was shorter with C-MAC D-blade VL compared with flexible FOB for awake nasotracheal intubation in anticipated diJicult airway. However, flexible FOB had better first attempt success and provides better visualization of the glottic opening. Hence, in anticipated diJicult nasal intubation a videolaryngoscope represents an acceptable alternative to fibreoptic intubation.
