A1  - Lee J.R.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - BACKGROUND: Both Total Intravenous Anesthesia (TIVA) and Inhaled anesthesia are commonly used anesthetic methods. Previously, we demonstrated that TIVA shows better quality of recovery score than inhalation anesthesia in female patients with thyroid cancer receiving thyroidectomy1. For surgeries more invasive than thyroidectomy, such as surgeries for pancreatic cancer or common bile duct (CBD) cancer, better quality of recovery seems to be imperative for further adjuvant therapies. In this prospective, randomized trial, we aimed to compare the quality of recovery between TIVA and inhalation anesthesia in pancreatic and CBD cancer patients METHODS: Total of 50 patients who were diagnosed with pancreatic cancer or CBD cancer and scheduled to undergo a pylorus-preserving pancreaticoduodenectomy (PPPD) or distal pancreatectomy (DP) surgery were enrolled for this double-blind, randomized trial. As 6 patients were excluded (5 patients had open and closure, 1 patient was sent to ICU with intubation at POD2 due to aggravated dyspnea), the total of 44 patients were analyzed. All patients were randomly allocated to either DES group (balanced anesthesia with desflurane inhalation and remifentanil infusion) or TIVA group (effect site controlled infusion with propofol and remifentanil, total intravenous anesthesia). The QOR-40 questionnaire was conducted 4 times, on the day of pre-operation (pre-op.), post-operation day 1,3 and 7 (POD 1,3,7). The primary endpoint was the score of QOR-40 on POD 3. Categorical and ordinal data were compared using chi-square test, and normally distributed data were compared using twosample independent t-test. P-value < 0.05 was considered statistically significant. RESULT(S): The mean global score of QOR-40 on POD3 was 177 in the TIVA group and 165.2 in the DES group, showing significantly better quality of recovery in the TIVA group (p=0.025). Among the five dimensions of the QOR- 40, emotional status, physical comfort and psychological support were significantly better in the TIVA group (p=0.028, 0.021, 0.027, respectively). The mean global score of QOR-40 on POD1 was similar at the both group (161.1 vs 161.9, p=0.873). The mean global score of QOR- 40 on POD7 was higher in the TIVA group than DES group (183.7 vs 179.8, respectively), but showed no statistical significance (p=0.355). CONCLUSION(S): In this study, the quality of recovery in patients who underwent PPPD or DP was better with TIVA than inhalation anesthesia at POD 3. Further study with larger number of patients and longer follow up to analyzes recurrence, metastasis and survival are needed. (Table Presented).
