T1  - SHOULD LABORATORY TESTS BE OBTAINED BEFORE OUTPATIENT GASTROINTESTINAL ENDOSCOPIC PROCEDURES IN CANCER PATIENTS?
T3  - 2019 Annual Meeting of the International Anesthesia Research Society and International Science Symposium. Montreal, QC Canada.
A1  - Nguyen L.
A1  - Hagan K.
A1  - Hancher-Hodges S.
A1  - Kwater P.
A1  - Rubin L.
A1  - Parikh S.K.
A1  - Cata J.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - BACKGROUND: A routine test is defined by the American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) as 'a test ordered in the absence of a specific indication or purpose'. An indicated test, however, is defined as 'a test that is ordered for a specific clinical indication or purpose'.1 Previous studies involving ambulatory patients undergoing surgery show that routine preoperative tests do not decrease perioperative adverse events.2-6 The patient population at our tertiary cancer center, however, may be more likely to have comorbidities and abnormal laboratory values that would compromise their safety than patients in a typical outpatient endoscopy practice. Our objective is to compare the demographics and outcomes between patients with laboratory variables recorded and patients whose laboratory data were not collected for patients undergoing outpatient endoscopies under general anesthesia (propofol iv infusion with or without intubation and inhalational agents) in the gastroenterology suite. We hypothesize that routine preoperative laboratory tests are unnecessary for a safe outpatient endoscopic procedure under general anesthesia at a cancer hospital. METHOD(S): With Institutonal Review Board approval, retrospective data was collected on all adult patients undergoing outpatient endoscopies in the gastroenterology suite under general anesthesia between June 16, 2015 and January 15, 2017. Patient demographic includes age, gender, race, body mass index, ASA status. Preoperative lab results within 1 month prior to the procedure, mortality 30 days post procedure are collected. Consults, cardiac enzymes, chest x-rays, abdominal x-rays, brain MRI or CT, hospital admissions, and emergency center visits within 7 days of the procedure are also collected. Fisher's exact test was used to assess the association between categorical and continuous variables. RESULT(S): Out of 2959 total patients, 1770 patients had laboratory tests, and 1189 patients did not have any laboratory tests. As presented in table 1 and 2, there were no statistically significant differences between the two groups with respect to demographic, 30 day mortality, and other outcomes. Other outcomes analyzed include consults, troponins, brain CT or MRI, chest X-ray findings, KUB findings, admissions and emergency visits 7 days post procedures. CONCLUSION(S): The retrospective analysis found no significant differences between patients who had routine laboratory tests and those who did not have laboratory tests with respect to the demographic factors or outcomes. Previous studies involving ambulatory patients undergoing surgery show that routine preoperative laboratory tests do not decrease perioperative adverse events. This analysis shows that this finding is also applicable to patients at a tertiary cancer hospital. Indicated laboratory testing rather than routine blood work should be performed. Eliminating unnecessary testing can represent significant cost savings, without jeopardizing patient safety. The limitation of this analysis is the small sample size of 2,959 patients. In addition, it is possible that patients who experienced complications after the procedure received follow up care at an outside hospital, and thus the adverse outcome is not recorded in our study. Future randomized controlled trial with larger sample size is warranted.
