T1  - Effects of subanesthetic ketamine on breathing and electroencephalography in intubated, spontaneously breathing ICU patients
T3  - 2020 Annual Meeting of the International Anesthesia Research Society, IARS 2020. San Francisco, CA United States.
A1  - Munoz-Acuna R.
A1  - Santer P.
A1  - Hammer M.
A1  - Rumyantsev S.
A1  - Kato S.
A1  - Berra L.
A1  - Eikermann M.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - INTRODUCTION: During weaning from mechanical ventilation, the use of respiratory depressants, such as propofol, benzodiazepines and opioids, is often required to achieve anxiolysis and pain control. Preclinical data suggest that low-dose ketamine, unlike other anesthetics, stimulates breathing.1 In this pharmaco-physiological interaction trial, we tested whether low-dose ketamine stimulates breathing while providing sedation.2 Hypothesis: Continuous infusion of ketamine at a subanesthetic dose is associated with increased inspiratory flow, as well as a ketamine-typical increase in EEG gamma power. METHOD(S): Adult patients admitted to a surgical ICU at two academic medical centers in Boston, MA, who required mechanical ventilation, were deemed suitable to undergo spontaneous breathing trials, and were maintained on a constant infusion rate of propofol were included. Eligible patients received a subanesthetic infusion of ketamine at 5 mcg/kg/min for 1hr (low dose) followed by 10 mcg/kg/min for 1hr (high dose). Measurements were performed during brief spontaneous breathing trials (1 min duration) at baseline and hours 1, 2 and 5. Respiratory flow and pressure were recorded using a pneumotachometer and pressure transducer, respectively. Changes in the EEG power spectrum derived from four frontal electrodes were recorded using a Masimo SedLine monitor. Statistical comparisons were performed using ANOVA. Changes in EEG gamma-power were analyzed using an established empirical bootstrap approach3. RESULT(S): A total of 12 patients were enrolled; 11 patients were included in the analysis. Ketamine increased maximum inspiratory flow (mean+/-SEM) by 10.55%+/-6.16% at low-dose and 9.89%+/-8.12% at highdose infusion compared to baseline (p=0.038). Minute volume ventilation remained unchanged. The I:E ratio (MEAN+/-SEM) increased from 0.53+/-0.05 at baseline to 0.56+/-0.05 and 0.60+/-0.05 at low- and high-dose infusion, respectively. (p=0.009). EEG gamma power (29-42 Hz) was increased (p<0.05) when compared to baseline, indicating typical power spectrum changes of ketamine. CONCLUSION(S): In mechanically ventilated, spontaneously breathing ICU patients, subanesthetic doses of ketamine during propofol sedation increased maximum inspiratory airflow and IE ratio while also increasing the gamma spectral power similarly to the reported effects of ketamine-induced unconsciousness. These results confirm that ketamine has respiratory stimulating properties and might represent an alternative to other sedating medications such as propofol, opioids or benzodiazepines when used in the ICU setting. (Figure Presented).
