T1  - Using diaphragmatic displacement ultrasonography to assess the recovery from neuromuscular blockade
T3  - 2020 Annual Meeting of the International Anesthesia Research Society, IARS 2020. San Francisco, CA United States.
A1  - Yin Y.
A1  - Chen X.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - INTRODUCTION: The residual neuromuscular blockade (RNMB) is associated with more critical respiratory events and higher postoperative morbidity after general anesthesia. At present, the commonly used indicators of the recovery from neuromuscular blockade are clinical signs and the train-of-four ratio (TOFr) of the adductor pollicis >0.9. These indexes couldn't reflect the function of diaphragm, which is the principle respiratory muscle. Sonographic evaluation of the diaphragm has been used to predict the outcome after extubation in the ICU. Diaphragmatic ultrasound is a noninvasive, easily performed, yet a reliable tool in assessing diaphragm excursion. The aim of the study is to evaluate the feasibility of assessing RNMB by diaphragmatic displacement (DD) using diaphragm ultrasonography (DU). METHOD(S): This study was performed following approval by the ethics committee of local hospital, and its registration number was ChiCTR1900021095. All eligible patients have signed informed consent. One hundred fifty patients in ASA physical status I and II, aged between 18-65 years, requiring general anesthesia for elective surgery were randomized into 2 groups of 75 each. General anesthesia was standardized in all patients. Intravenous induction was performed with fentanyl 2-4ug/kg, propofol 2-2.5mg/kg, cis-atracurium 0.2mg/kg. Orotracheal intubation was done after induction. Anesthesia was maintained with sevoflurane, remifentanil and propofol, keeping BIS between 40-60. At the end of surgery, patients were extubated, when they met following criteria: (1) being awake and execute simple commands; (2) sustained head lift or hand grip for more than 5s; (3) respiratory pattern is regular with a tidal volume of 6-7 mL/kg referred to ideal body weight. Patients were randomly divided to 2 groups depending on whether were given muscular blockade antagonists or not. The patient was then transferred to PACU when the anesthetist was approving with their clinical status. According to the modified Aldrete scoring system, patients discharged from PACU when they met the following criteria :(1) PaCO2<=45mmHg;(2) able to breathe deeply and cough freely;(3) able to maintain O2 saturation >92% on room air;(4) fully awake and able to move voluntarily or on command. Demographic data, intraoperative details, perioperative variables were recorded. The DD and the TOFr were measured before the induction of general anesthesia (baseline), one minute after extubation, and before being discharged from the PACU. The right hemidiaphragm was examined using 2-dimensional and M-mode ultrasonography during quite breathing. The primary objective is to compare the difference in DD between antagonized and unantagonized patients and the incidence of hypoxemia after extubation. The RNMB is defined as TOFr < 0.9 and hypoxemia is defined as SpO2 <93% with or without supplemental oxygen administration in PACU. Data analysis was performed using SPSS version 22. DD and TOFr was presented as mean with standard deviation, independent-samples t-test used to compare the two groups. The incidence of RNMB and hypoxemia were presented as proportions and Chi square test or Fisher exact test used to compare the two groups. P values less than 0.05 were considered statistically significant. RESULT(S): The DD in the antagonized group at 1 minute after extubation was significantly larger than that in the unantagonized group (14.14+/-1.93 vs 12.96+/-1.62 mm, p<0.05). When discharged from PACU, the TOFr of antagonized group was markedly higher than that of the unantagonized group (94.40+/-7.55% vs 90.34+/-11.24%, p=0.01), however the DD was not remarkably different between the two groups (13.01+/-1.68 vs 12.93+/-2.40, p=0.831). The incidence of RNMB when discharged from PACU was 20% (15/75) in the antagonized group and 35% (26/75) in the unantagonized group (p=0.044), though all patients reach the criteria of discharging from PACU. Hypoxemia after extubation occurred 6.7% (5/75) in the antagonized group, and 9.3% (7/75) in the unantagonized group (p=0.765). CONCLUSION(S): The diaphragmatic displacement is more accurate for assessing the function of respiration after neuromuscular block. Diaphragm ultrasonography could be a helpful and accurate tool to assess diaphragmatic function and the recovery from neuromuscular blockade (Table Presented).
