T1  - Limited English proficiency is not associated with sedation practices in intubated, critically ill patients
T3  - 2020 Annual Meeting of the International Anesthesia Research Society, IARS 2020. San Francisco, CA United States.
A1  - Vu T.-G.
A1  - Pirracchio R.
A1  - Whitlock E.L.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - INTRODUCTION: Limited English proficiency (LEP) is associated with disparities in health care, ranging from poor access to primary care, to increased adverse maternal health outcomes(1,2,3). In critical care, however, English proficiency is associated with a decrease in mortality independent of race(4). It is unclear, though, what effect communication and language may have on sedation. We performed a singleinstitution retrospective study of ICU propofol sedation dose in mechanically ventilated patients with respect to English proficiency at a tertiary academic medical center, hypothesizing that due to language barriers, and possibly through cultural barriers, non- or limited-English speakers receive less sedation than English speakers despite the non-verbal nature of intubation. METHOD(S): We included 1648 adults (18 and older) who were intubated (first episode during hospitalization) and mechanically ventilated at our center between June 1, 2015 and June 1, 2018, after exclusions for neurologic deficits/diagnoses (n=465), DNR/DNI status (n=190), and administration of dexmedetomidine (n=343), neuromuscular blockade (n=309), high-dose benzodiazepines (n=14), and high-dose opioids (n=30). The primary predictor was English language proficiency, as documented in our electronic medical record (EMR) by language preference. The primary outcome was propofol dosage (mean mcg/kg/min for each day of ventilation) for up to 7 days after initial intubation. We modeled propofol dose using linear mixed effects regression adjusting for sex, race, age, relationship status, SOFA score, insurance status, BMI, and oral morphine equivalents (OMEs), including participantlevel random slopes and intercepts. RESULT(S): 13.45% (222/1652) were identified as LEP. There was no impact of LEP on average hourly propofol dosage (-0.052 mcg/kg/min, 95% confidence interval, -6.419 to 6.315, P=0.987). There also was no significant interaction between race and LEP on propofol dosage. Propofol dosage was significantly affected by opioid dose (0.104 mcg/kg/min per additional OME, 95% confidence interval, 0.047 to 0.161, P=0.000). CONCLUSION(S): We found no significant association between propofol dosage and LEP status at our institution. Our analysis was limited by inclusion of a medically heterogeneous cohort and inability to account for spontaneous awakening trials; however, the effect size estimate was relatively narrow, and large differences in propofol administration based on English proficiency are unlikely. Generalizability of this finding is unclear, as our hospital employs a large number of bilingual providers and may not be representative of national care practices. EMR-based data analysis may be a viable avenue by which medical centers can assess for disparities in care provided.
