T1  - First Case Of Bilateral Lung Transplantation For E-Cigarette Associated Lung Injury
T3  - SCA 41st Annual Meeting and Workshops and 8th Annual Thoracic Anesthesia Symposium (TAS). Chicago, IL United States.
A1  - Parikh S.
A1  - Marouki M.
A1  - Williams D.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - Introduction: Electronic cigarettes (E-Cigarette) or "vape pens" are hand-held devices used to inhale aerosolized substances including nicotine and THC. There has been a surge in the usage of these devices among teenagers since 2014. The prevalence of e- cigarette use has increased further among the U.S. high school students from 11.7% to 20.8%, during the 2017-2018 period 1. Recently, there has been an up-trending number of cases reported for vaping-associated pulmonary injury, in which the mechanism of injury is mainly unknown. However, a pathological case series from Mayo Clinic suggests that lung injury in these cases is from toxic substances causing airway centered pneumonitis 2. Case Presentation: We present a 16-year-old previously healthy male who was admitted for worsening shortness of breath and cough. A cardiac workup including EKG, cardiac enzymes and Transthoracic Echocardiogram (TTE) were normal. A thoracic CT scan for pulmonary embolus was negative. Infectious disease workup including Legionella antigen urine, BAL Gram stain, AFB stain and fungal stain and PCP were all negative. With unclear etiology, he was subsequently treated for community acquired and atypical pneumonia. Serial chest x-rays revealed subcutaneous emphysema and pneumomediastinum. The patient was intubated 6 days into his hospital admission due to hypoxemia and was started on high-dose IV steroids. Due to worsening hypoxemia, the patient was given a trial of inhaled nitric oxide, however, no improvement was noted. Subsequently, Veno-Venous Extracorporeal Membrane Oxygenation (VV ECMO) was placed and initiated. The patient was transferred to our institution for pulmonary transplant work up. Approximately six weeks after month and half into his presentation the patient underwent bilateral lung transplantation under the usage of Veno-Arterial (VA) ECMO with the assistance of Transesophageal Echocardiogram (TEE) and a PA Catheter. During this patient's intraoperative course, he required high doses of midazolam, fentanyl, ketamine, and propofol infusions along with 1 MAC of volatile anesthetic to maintain adequate anesthesia and analgesia. The patient was given multiple blood products and colloids to address hypovolemia and to allow for adequate flow for the ECMO device during the procedure. The intraoperative post transplantation TEE revealed a moderately reduced right ventricular function requiring epinephrine infusion. The patient was subsequently weaned off of VA ECMO in the cardiothoracic intensive care unit (CTICU) while on epinephrine infusion and transferred to the regular nursing floor less than one week later. Discussion(s): Several different environmental agents are responsible for lung parenchymal injury. In late August 2019, the CDC reported 215 cases of severe lung injury related to vaporizer use 3. A number of these cases were treated with a 7-day course of steroids. We report the first case of E-Cigarette associated lung injury that required bilateral lung transplantation.
