T1  - A Case of Refractory Electrical Storm Successfully Treated with Propofol Infusion
T3  - International Anesthesia Research Society Annual Meeting, IARS 2024. Seattle, WA United States.
A1  - Sorensen M.
A1  - Briggs J.
A1  - Wagener B.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Introduction: Electrical storm is a growing concern of life threatening illness. The most common definition is 3 or more episodes of ventricular tachycardia, ventricular fibrillation, or implanted cardioverter-defibrillator within the span of 24 hours. [1] A direct cause is known in less than 10% of total cases, and most commonly there are no clear inciting factors. There has not been shown any clinical predictors of electrical storm [2]. Of concern, electrical storm is a known predictor of mortality after occurrence [3]. Treatment historically has consisted of beta blockade, antiarrhythmic medications, and overdrive pacing. The following case describes an occurrence of electrical storm refractory to most traditional measures, ultimately broken by the use of propofol. Method(s): Medically Challenging Case: Patient is a 56 year old female with past medical history of coronary artery disease, ICD placement, atrial fibrillation, hyperlipidemia, hypothyroidism, and tobacco abuse among others. She reports taking mexiletine at home along with her other medications. Patient presented to the ER with chief complaint of chest pain and defibrillators from her ICD. Patient was found to have an inferior myocardial infarction on her initial EKG, and she was subsequently brought to the cath lab for stent placement with successful reconstitution of flow. During this time period and thereafter patient began to experience runs of polymorphic ventricular tachycardia. Patient was placed on amiodarone and lidocaine drips. In the cardiac ICU, the patient continued to have multiple ventricular tachycardia runs with over 5 resulting in defibrillation while in the unit. Electrophysiology was consulted, and procainamide was added for additional therapy. On day 3 since admission to the hospital, the patient continued to have multiple runs of mono and polymorphic ventricular tachycardia. Recommendations were made for sedation of this patient and a trial of propranolol which also failed to improve her now diagnosis of electrical storm. The decision to intubate and sedate the patient was executed. She was sedated with propofol and fentanyl. Serial EKGs were shown to have prolonging QTc intervals secondary to antiarrhythmic management. The decision was made to halt her antiarrhythmics due to concern for prolonged QTc, and the patient was left only on sedation as of day 3 of her admission. Going forward, the patient presented with no further episodes of ventricular dysrhythmias and she was extubated without incident on day 4. The patient was discharged on day 5 of her admission in excellent shape, and her home meds plus amiodarone were resumed. Result(s): Not Applicable - Medically Challenging Case Report Conclusion(s): This case aims to highlight the use of propofol in the treatment of refractory electrical storm. The usual approach to therapy includes antiarrhythmics (such as amiodarone, lidocaine, procainamide, etc.), overdrive pacing when tolerated, and ultimately sedation for refractory cases. Anesthesia as a specialty is known for its favorable use of propofol for the induction of anesthesia, but this case aims to highlight its use outside of the operating room or even for sedation. Evidence is currently suggestive that the sympathetic blockade from propofol can be incredibly useful in cases of refractory electrical storm as seen in this case [4]. (Figure Presented).
