T1  - Effect of Dexmedetomidine on Optic Nerve Sheath Diameter Changes in Laproscopic Hysterectomy; A Randomized Placebo Control Trial
A1  - Kumar P.
A1  - Prakash A.
A1  - Hussain M.
A1  - Akhileshwar
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Background: Dexmedetomidine (DEX) is a highly selective 2-agonist that provides sedation that is similar to that of natural sleep, anxiolysis, sympatholysis, and an anesthetic-sparing effect without causing clinically significant respiratory depression. These effects are all achieved without the use of barbiturates. The 2 adrenoceptors in the locus coeruleus of the central nervous system are responsible for the sedative effect that this substance has. It has been demonstrated that administering a single intraoperative dosage without following it up with an infusion can lessen the need for postoperative analgesics, facilitate a more seamless transition out of anesthesia, and extend the effects of regional anesthesia. The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) in the United States has given its current approval for the use of dexmedetomidine (brand name: Precedex) in the sedation of intubated adults via intravenous bolus and continuous infusion for up to 24 hours, as well as the sedation of non-intubated patients prior to or during surgical procedures and other procedures. DEX (Dexdor) was granted permission in 2011 by the European Union to be used for the indefinitely prolonged sedation of adult patients in intensive care units. Even though there is a substantial body of research supporting the use of DEX in paediatric anesthesia, the practice is still considered "off-label." Off-label uses of a medication refer to any applications of that medication that are not included on the product's prescription label. These include variations in dose, patient category, and the intended usage of the medication. Patients who are pregnant or children are more likely to be given drugs that are not approved for their condition by the manufacturer. Off-label use of a medication is permissible under certain circumstances, including when there is no other treatment option that would be more appropriate and when a doctor is certain that they are employing the medication in a manner that is in line with the most recent consensus in the medical community.Copyright © 2024, Dr. Yashwant Research Labs Pvt. Ltd.. All rights reserved.
