N2  - Introduction: Current data is limited to describe the types of agents used for intubation following traumatic brain injury. As demonstrated in the literature, there exist various strategies for intubation with little standardization of practice. In securing the airway, it is necessary to control against increases in ICP while preventing significant hypotension or hypertension. Furthermore, efficiently and expediently gaining a secure airway is critical to prevent prolonged hypercapnia, hypoxia, and to protect against risk of aspiration. This study explores the usage of induction and paralytic agents for the intubation of TBI patients in multiple centers in the US. Method(s): The Transforming Clinical Research and Knowledge in TBI (TRACK-TBI) Study database was used to investigate medications administered in the intubation of adults (age >17) with moderate-severe TBI in ICUs at 18 level 1, U.S. trauma centers. Descriptive statistics were used to examine induction and paralytic agents used for early intubation (in the emergency department or within the first day of ICU admission) among critically ill patients with moderate-severe TBI. Result(s): Among 341 patients, after excluding events for unclear documentation, 87 intubation events were recorded. Of the intubation events, 44/87 (50.6%) utilized propofol, 47/87 (54.0%) utilized etomidate, 8/87 (9.2%) utilized ketamine, and 12/87 (13.8%) utilized midazolam. 56/87 (64.4%) utilized rocuronium, 36/87 (41.4%) utilized succinylcholine, 7/87 (8.0%) utilized cisatracurium, and 3/87 (3.4%) utilized vecuronium. 22/87 (25.3%) utilized 2 induction agents concurrently. 11 utilized propofol and etomidate. 5 utilized propofol and midazolam. 3 utilized ketamine and midazolam. 2 utilized ketamine and etomidate. 1 utilized propofol and ketamine. 16/87 (18.4%) utilized 2 intubation agents concurrently. 9 utilized succinylcholine and rocuronium. 3 utilized succinylcholine and cisatracurium. 2 utilized rocuronium and cisatracurium. 1 utilized succinylcholine and vecuronium. 1 utilized rocuronium and vecuronium. Conclusion(s): There exist various induction and paralytic strategies in intubating patients with moderate-severe TBI. Future studies should aim to examine the association of induction strategies with functional outcomes and morbidity/ mortality outcomes can be further observed.
