T1  - COMPARING KETAMINE AND ETOMIDATE AS INDUCTION AGENTS FOR ENDOTRACHEAL INTUBATION IN CRITICALLY ILL ADULT PATIENTS: AN UPDATED BAYESIAN META-ANALYSIS UPDATE
T3  - CHEST 2024 Annual Meeting. Boston Convention and Exhibition Center, Boston United States.
A1  - Akbar U.
A1  - Lohana A.B.H.I.
A1  - Kumar B.
A1  - Shafique N.
A1  - Muhibullah F.N.U.
A1  - Umer A.
A1  - Rizwan U.M.E.R.
A1  - Rehman U.
A1  - Hussain R.I.D.A.
A1  - Sajjad R.
A1  - Malik M.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - SESSION TITLE: Breath of Freedom: Unlocking Ventilator Weaning Strategies SESSION TYPE: Original Investigations PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2024 08:00 am - 09:00 am PURPOSE: Ketamine is considered to have a positive impact on cardiovascular stability and is also considered as a safer option improving patient outcomes. This meta-analysis evaluates whether using ketamine, as opposed to alternative medications, decreases the risk of death in critically ill patients who need intubation. METHOD(S): We conducted a comprehensive literature search through MEDLINE, Embase, and the Cochrane Library from their start dates up to March 1st, 2024. We aimed to find randomized controlled trials and matched observational studies that compared the use of ketamine with any other control agent in critically ill patients for induction during intubation. Our primary focus was on assessing mortality rates at the longest available follow-up period. Secondary measures included the Sequential Organ Failure Assessment score (SOFA), the number of ventilator-free days and vasopressor-free days by day 28, post-induction mean arterial pressure, and the rate of successful intubation on the first attempt. For our main analysis on mortality, we employed a Bayesian random-effects meta-analysis approach, using a risk ratio (RR) scale. Secondary outcomes were analyzed using a frequentist random-effects model. This study was registered with the Open Science Framework for transparency and reproducibility. R Studio was used for analysis. RESULT(S): Our results involving 9 studies indicate that while there is an observable trend towards reduced event rates with Ketamine, the data do not provide conclusive evidence of a statistically significant reduction in mortality (Intercept mean = -1.4, 95% Credible Interval [CrI] from -1.9 to -0.9). Using a frequentist random-effects model, the meta-analysis shows there was no statistically significant difference found in the secondary outcomes; SOFA score change, number of ventilator and vasopressor-free days, post-induction mean arterial pressure, and rate of successful intubation on the first attempt. CONCLUSION(S): Our meta-analysis reveals that while ketamine may offer a trend towards improved outcomes in critically ill patients requiring intubation, it does not conclusively reduce mortality or significantly impact secondary clinical outcomes. Further research is needed to definitively ascertain its benefits. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: The analysis hints at the potential advantages of using ketamine for intubation in critical care settings, though its impact on mortality and other key clinical metrics remains inconclusive. Ongoing research is essential to fully understanding its role and efficacy. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Usman Akbar No disclosure on file for Rida Hussain No relevant relationships by Besham Kumar No relevant relationships by Abhi Lohana No relevant relationships by Muhammad Malik No relevant relationships by Fnu Muhibullah No relevant relationships by Urooj Rehman No relevant relationships by Umer Rizwan No relevant relationships by Ribal Sajjad No relevant relationships by Nouman Shafique No relevant relationships by Ahmed UmerCopyright © 2024 American College of Chest Physicians
