T1  - KETAMINE-MIDAZOLAM VS FENTANYL-MIDAZOLAM COMBINATION AS INDUCTION AGENTS FOR TRACHEAL INTUBATION IN CRITICALLY ILL CHILDREN: A PILOT, FEASIBILITY, OPEN LABEL, RANDOMIZED CONTROLLED TRIAL
T3  - 12th Congress of the World Federation of Pediatric Intensive and Critical Care Societies, WFPICCS 2024. Cancun Mexico.
A1  - Tungal S.
A1  - Sankar J.
A1  - Jat K.R.
A1  - Kabra S.K.
A1  - Lodha R.
Y1  - 2024//
N2  - Aims & Objectives: Large retrospective studies have shown reduced likelihood of adverse hemodynamic events associated with tracheal intubation with the use of ketamine during induction. However, prospective studies evaluating various agents are lacking. We compared the incidence of adverse events during tracheal intubation when ketamine-midazolam combination is used as induction regimen compared to midazolam-fentanyl combination in critically ill children. Method(s): A pilot, open-label, randomized controlled trial was conducted from July 2021 to August 2023 among critically ill children aged 2 months to 18 years requiring intubation. Intervention group received ketamine and midazolam combination while the control group received fentanyl and midazolam combination before intubation. Primary outcome was incidence of adverse hemodynamic events and secondary outcomes were other adverse events, need for neuromuscular blocker, need for repeat doses of study drugs. Result(s): Ninety-two children were enrolled in the study (47 and 45 in intervention and control groups, respectively). Incidence of adverse hemodynamic events was similar in both the groups [9 (19.1%) vs 12 (26.6%); RR- 0.72; 95% CI (0.33-1.24)]. Incidence of other adverse events was comparable [13 (27.6%) vs. 16 (35.5%); RR- 0.78; 95%CI (0.42-1.35)]. Proportion of children requiring neuromuscular blockade [5 (10.6%) vs. 8 (17.7%); RR-0.60; 95%CI (0.21- 1.69)] and repeat doses of trial drug to achieve adequate sedation-analgesia [16 (34%) vs. 20 (44.4%); RR- 0.77; 95% CI (0.46 -1.28)] were also similar. Conclusion(s): No statistically significant differences were observed between the groups in both the adverse events. However, the pilot study provides framework to conduct adequately powered larger studies.
