T1  - Low Dose Intravenous Dexmedetomidine is Effective in Attenuating Haemodynamic Stress Response Associated with Laryngoscopy and Intubation-Prospective Randomised Double Blinded Study
A1  - Chandrappa P.
A1  - Paramesh S.M.
A1  - Venkappa Y.
A1  - Gurudatta K.N.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Background and Objective: Laryngoscopy and intubation cause an intense increase in heart rate, blood pressure and serum concentration of catecholamines leading to hypertension, tachycardia and dysrhythmias. These changes lead to advertisements in susceptible individuals. Dexmedetomidine, a novel alpha 2 agonist, has numerous applications in anaesthesia and ICU because it causes sedation, hypnosis, analgesia and sympatholytic. Hence the present study is undertaken to evaluate the efficacy of 0.5 microg/kg dexmedetomidine on haemodynamic responses to direct laryngoscopy and tracheal intubation. Also induction dose of propofol. Methodology: 100 subjects of Age group-18-60 years, of either sex.; ASA grade I & II admitted for elective surgeries from various departments of McGANN Teaching District Hospital were included after obtaining clearance from Institutional Ethical Committee. The study population were randomly divided into Group D [Dexmedetomidine group] (n=50) and Group S [Saline group] (n=50) received Dexmedetomidine 0.5 microg/kg and saline respectively. SBP, DBP, MAP, Heart rate, SpO2 were recorded at 1 min (T5), 3min (T6), 5min (T7), 10min (T8), 15 min (T9) and 30 minutes (T10) after laryngoscopy & intubation. Result(s): Group D showed statistically significant fall in the hemodynamic response after laryngoscopy and intubation compared to group S; it also showed decrease in induction dose of propofol. Conclusion(s): Dexmedetomidine in a dose of 0.5microg/kg body weight given over 10 minutes,10 minutes before induction significantly attenuates hemodynamic stress response to laryngoscopy and endotracheal intubation without any significant side effects.Copyright © 2023, Dr Yashwant Research Labs Pvt Ltd. All rights reserved.
