T1  - KETAMINE TO ASSIST VENTILATOR WEANING AND EXTUBATION IN PATIENTS WHO FAIL SBT FROM SEVERE AGITATION AND ANXIETY
T3  - CHEST 2023 Annual Meeting. Honolulu United States.
A1  - Richardson C.C.
A1  - Lantigua Tatem L.U.I.S.
A1  - Russell D.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Report Posters 54 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/11/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Ketamine is a dissociative anesthetic widely used for procedural sedation, rapid sequence induction for endotracheal intubation and during general anesthesia. There is also promising research data for many other uses, including in the treatment for chronic pain and major depression disorder. Agitation and anxiety are encountered frequently in the ICU and may delay ventilator weaning and liberation. Those with mental health conditions have shown to often have more severe agitation and anxiety that may prolong their days on the ventilator and in the ICU. The current data is sparse examining the use of ketamine in the ventilator weaning and extubation process, however, there have been several studies showing favorable ventilation effects such as increased inspiratory flows and reduced inspiratory work of breathing. We present a case of a 38-year-old female with major depression and post-traumatic stress disorders who was able to be successfully liberated from the ventilator with the use of a one-time 0.35 mg/kg intravenous ketamine infusion, despite multiple failed spontaneous breathing trial attempts, due to severe agitation and anxiety. CASE PRESENTATION: A 38-year-old female was admitted to the medical ICU after a suicide attempt from an overdose of quetiapine, valproic acid, and amphetamines. She had history of major depressive disorder and post-traumatic stress disorder, as well as prior suicide attempts. The patient was intubated on hospital day (HD) 3 for worsening hypoxemia, found to also have MRSA pneumonia. She suffered cardiac arrest peri-intubation and achieved return of spontaneous circulation after one round of ACLS. Her course was complicated by prolonged intubation and difficulty with ventilator weaning. On HD 12 (ventilator day 9) patient was requiring minimal ventilator support but was persistently failing spontaneous breathing trials, due to severe agitation and rapid shallow breathing, despite the addition of dexmedetomidine. On HD 15 (ventilator day 12) a one-time 25 mg (0.35 mg/kg) infusion of intravenous ketamine was administered that evening. The patient's respiratory mechanics and tolerance of pressure support ventilation, as well as her affect and anxiety, appeared much improved. The next morning, she passed a SBT for the first time and was successfully extubated. The patient did not require re-intubation and made a full recovery from a pulmonary standpoint. She was later transferred to an inpatient psychiatric unit for further care. DISCUSSION(S): We present the case of a 38-year-old suicidal female with severe agitation, which presented a challenge for extubation. Dexmedetomidine has been shown to aid in the complex extubation process in patients with severe agitation by facilitating extubation and increasing ventilator-free time. In this case we propose Ketamine likely had a similar effect by facilitating this patient's weaning process. In intubated patients, ketamine increased inspiratory flow and reduced inspiratory work of breathing. Ketamine has also been correlated to improve the transition to extubation with less coughing, laryngospasm, and vomiting. CONCLUSION(S): Ketamine should be considered as an agent to facilitate in weaning and extubation in ICU patients with anxiety and agitation. REFERENCE #1: Suleiman A, Santer P, Munoz-Acuna R, Hammer M, Schaefer MS, Wachtendorf LJ, Rumyantsev S, Berra L, Chamadia S, Johnson-Akeju O, Baedorf-Kassis EN, Eikermann M. Effects of Ketamine Infusion on Breathing and Encephalography in Spontaneously Breathing ICU Patients. J Intensive Care Med. 2023 Mar;38(3):299-306. doi: 10.1177/08850666221119716. Epub 2022 Aug 8. PMID: 35934953. REFERENCE #2: Reade MC, Eastwood GM, Bellomo R, Bailey M, Bersten A, Cheung B, Davies A, Delaney A, Ghosh A, van Haren F, Harley N, Knight D, McGuiness S, Mulder J, O'Donoghue S, Simpson N, Young P; DahLIA Investigators; Australian and New Zealand Intensive Care Society Clinical Trials Group. Effect of Dexmedetomidine Added to Standard Care on Ventilator-Free Time in Patients With Agitated Delirium: A Randomized Clinical Trial. JAMA. 2016 Apr 12;315(14):1460-8. doi: 10.1001/jama.2016.2707. Erratum in: JAMA. 2016 Aug 16;316(7):775. PMID: 26975647. REFERENCE #3: Mohammad Khalil A, Makram Botros J, Boules ML, Gaber Ragab S. Reliable and Rapid Smooth Extubation After "Ketamine-Propfol Mixture" for Induction of General Anesthesia in Laparoscopic Drilling of Polycystic Ovary: A Randomized, Double-blind, Comparative Study. Anesth Pain Med. 2021 Apr 26;11(2):e113919. doi: 10.5812/aapm.113919. PMID: 34336631; PMCID: PMC8314091. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Luis Lantigua Tatem No relevant relationships by Christopher Richardson No relevant relationships by Derek RussellCopyright © 2023 American College of Chest Physicians
