T1  - Surgical Levels of Propofol Anesthesia Disrupt REM Sleep Homeostasis in Aged Mice
T3  - 51st Annual Meeting of the Society for Neuroscience in Anesthesiology and Critical Care, SNACC 2023. Alexandria, VA United States.
A1  - Jedrusiak M.
A1  - Park J.
A1  - Davis E.
A1  - Atluri N.
A1  - Osuru H.
A1  - Lunardi N.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Background: Previous studies investigating the effects of propofol on sleep have yielded inconsistent findings. While sedation with propofol affects sleep homeostasis similarly to natural sleep in adult rats (Tung A. et al. Anesthesia and Analgesia, 2001; Anesthesiology, 2004), it does not eliminate a pre-existing sleep debt in Drosophila (Gardner B. et al. Anesthesiology, 2016). Additionally, patients admitted to the ICU experience almost complete loss of REM sleep during propofol sedation (Kondili E. et al. Intensive Care Med, 2012). In this study, we sought to resolve this long-standing controversy by systematically analyzing sleepwake activity and sleep structure in aged mice exposed to a surgical level of propofol anesthesia. APPROACH: To address the limitations of prior research, we used 20-month-old mice, comparable to human age 60- 70 years old, and obtained 24-hour post-anesthetic recordings to evaluate the extent of sleep disruption throughout a full sleep-wake cycle. We implanted electroencephalographic (EEG) and electromyographic (EMG) electrodes in 20 C57BL/6J mice and assigned them randomly to either a propofol or an intralipid group. Mice in the propofol group were intubated and given propofol through a commercially-implanted venous catheter, titrated to an EEG delta wave endpoint for 3 hours. Meanwhile, mice in the intralipid group breathed spontaneously and received an equal volume of 10% intralipid through a venous catheter. EEG/EMG recordings were taken in both groups at the end of the infusion period (Fig. 1). Result(s): Compared to the control group, propofol mice spent substantially less time in REM sleep during the initial 8 hours post-anes- thesia, followed by a REM sleep rebound between hours 16 and 24 (Fig. 2A). Total NREM sleep and the percentage of stage III NREM sleep were also significantly reduced during the first 8 hours post-propofol (Fig. 2B and C). Additionally, during the first 8 hours post-anesthesia, propofol mice spent more time being awake and a similar amount of time moving relative to controls (Fig. 2D and E), suggesting that they were unlikely to be still under the effects of propofol anesthesia. Conclusion(s): Under conditions similar to recovery from general anesthesia with propofol in elderly patients, mice experience a significant reduction in NREM sleep, particularly stage III, and a near-complete absence of REM sleep, followed by REM sleep rebound. These findings support previous research on the effects of propofol on NREM sleep, but indicate that propofol may have different effects on REMsleep than previously thought, i.e., propofol anesthesia does not satisfy the brain's need for REM sleep. (Table Presented).
