T1  - HELIOX IN ACUTE SEVERE ASTHMA: A CASE SERIES
T3  - CHEST 2023 Annual Meeting. Honolulu United States.
A1  - Krishna Boppana H.
A1  - Zaman M.
A1  - Sondhi D.S.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - SESSION TITLE: Asthma, Eosinophilia, and Cough SESSION TYPE: Rapid Fire Case Reports PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 09:40 am - 10:25 am INTRODUCTION: Heliox is a low density gas mixture of 70-80% helium and 20-30% oxygen and has been used in patients with acute severe asthma (ASA) with mixed results. We present 2 cases of ASA managed with heliox that resulted in improvement in peak pressures (Ppeak) and respiratory acidosis. CASE PRESENTATION: Case 1: A 40-year-old male with history of asthma presented with increased work of breathing. He was tachycardic and tachypneic with bilateral wheezing and decreased air movement. He was initiated on non-invasive ventilation (NIV) and received parenteral steroids, bronchodilators and magnesium but continued to have increased work of breathing and worsening acidosis. He was intubated, deeply sedated and paralyzed and was started on continuous albuterol, theophylline and ketamine. There was no improvement in acidosis and he continued to have severe air trapping and elevated Ppeak. He was started on heliox resulting in lower Ppeak (49 to 41 cmH2O) and improvement in respiratory acidosis (pH 7.10 to 7.21). Case 2: A 22-year-old female with history of asthma presented with severe respiratory distress. There was decreased air entry along with bilateral wheezing and she was initiated on NIV and received parenteral steroids, magnesium and bronchodilators without significant improvement in work of breathing and was therefore intubated, deeply sedated and paralyzed. Aminophylline and ketamine infusions were also started along with continuous albuterol but Ppeak remained high. Course complicated by pneumothorax requiring placement of a chest tube. She was started on heliox which resulted in lower peak pressures (50 to 43 cmH2O). DISCUSSION(S): Helium is a low-density gas and can therefore lead to decreased airflow resistance when used in combination with oxygen. This is achieved by facilitating greater laminar flow in large airways and at branch points in the tracheobronchial tree permitting increased ventilation (1). Patients with ASA, have severe airflow limitation, gas trapping and dynamic hyperinflation which leads to higher Ppeak and respiratory acidosis due to accumulation of carbon dioxide (2). Theoretically, heliox should benefit these patients as it would allow for increased ventilation and improvement in Ppeak. However, studies have showed mixed results with heliox use in patients with ASA. From our experience, use of heliox resulted in improvement in Ppeak in both patients and improvement in respiratory acidosis in one patient. While heliox is not used for treating ASA, it seems to be effective in reducing airway pressures giving disease-specific treatment such as steroids time to work. CONCLUSION(S): While the use of heliox in ASA in adults has been shown to have mixed results, we believe that a trial of heliox is warranted given our experience with using heliox in this patient group. Reference #1: Chevrolet JC. Helium oxygen mixtures in the intensive care unit. Crit Care. 2001 Aug;5(4):179-81. doi: 10.1186/cc1019. Epub 2001 Jul 13. Reference #2: Reuben AD, Harris AR. Heliox for asthma in the emergency department: a review of the literature. Emerg Med J. 2004 Mar;21(2):131-5. doi: 10.1136/emj.2002.003483. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Hemanth Krishna Boppana No relevant relationships by Damanpaul Sondhi No relevant relationships by Momina ZamanCopyright © 2023 American College of Chest Physicians
