A1  - Ramirez C.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Report Posters 73 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 02:10 pm - 02:55 pm INTRODUCTION: Polyuria is an uncommon presentation, particularly in critically ill patients. Defined as a urine output greater than three liters per day in adults, this syndrome can have multifactorial causes, with medications being common culprits. Although scarcely reported in literature, polyuria from dexmedetomidine-induced diabetes insipidus (DI) has been suggested as a cause of polyuria in critically ill patients. Here, we present a case of dexmedetomidine as an underreported cause of polyuria in a critical ill patient. CASE PRESENTATION: A 56 year-old-male with a history of type 2 diabetes mellitus on insulin, alcohol and polysubstance use disorder, chronic kidney disease stage II, hypertension, hyperlipidemia, was brought to the emergency department after being found unresponsive. He was intubated for airway protection after no response to naloxone administration, and transferred to the intensive care unit (ICU). A urine drug screen was positive for cocaine. Dexmedetomidine and midazolam were favored as sedatives over propofol due to hypertriglyceridemia. The patient's initial laboratory results showed a serum sodium of 134 mmol/L (136-146 mmol/L) and glucose of 156 g/dL (70-110 g/dL). His computed tomography of the head showed no acute intracranial hemorrhage, significant mass effect, or large evolving territorial infarct, and a stable ventricular prominence out of proportion to sulci concerning for communicating hydrocephalus versus central atrophy. He was also noted to exhibit polyuria with a urine output of approximately 10 liters over 24 hours and with a serum sodium of 147 mmol/L (136-146 mmol/L), serum osmolality (Osm) of 305mOSm/kg (280-300mOsm/kg), urine Osm 352mOsm/kg (38-1400mOsm/kg), and random urine sodium of 177 mmol/L (<20 mmol/L). With a high suspicion of DI, dexmedetomidine was discontinued. 24 hours later, polyuria decreased and sodium level returned to normal without water deprivation testing. He was later discharged in stable condition. DISCUSSION(S): 0.004% associated with the body's inability to effectively auto-regulate water balance, due to inadequate release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) or resistance to ADH receptors in the kidneys.[1] Dexmedetomidine is an alpha-2 adrenergic agonist commonly used as therapy to manage delirium and a sedative agent in critical care settings.[2] There have been cases reported in the literature of dexmedetomidine causing DI in perioperative conditions and only two cases reported on similar presentations in the ICU. Dexmedetomidine acts at the locus coeruleus, the area of the brain whose lesions have been classically associated with decreased ADH production, which may lead to central DI.[3] In our patient's case, an MRI of the brain would have been warranted to determine the state of the locus coeruleus, but his polyuria resolved after cessation of dexmedetomidine. CONCLUSION(S): Our case serves as a stark reminder for physicians to maintain a high index of suspicion for medication side effects like DI with dexmedetomidine, particularly in critical care settings where fluid and electrolyte shifts can be detrimental to patients. REFERENCE #1: Van Decar LM, Reynolds EG, Sharpe EE, Harbell MW, Kosiorek HE, Kraus MB. Perioperative Diabetes Insipidus Caused by Anesthetic Medications: A Review of the Literature. Anesth Analg. 2022 Jan 1;134(1):82-89. doi: 10.1213/ANE.0000000000005344. PMID: 33410610. REFERENCE #2: Mohammed M. Uddin, Joseph Sebastian, Muhammad Usama, Fazal I. Raziq, Ghulam Saydain, Noreen F. Rossi, "Dexmedetomidine Induced Polyuria in the Intensive Care Unit", Case Reports in Critical Care, vol. 2021, Article ID 8850116, 3 pages, 2021. https://doi.org/10.1155/2021/8850116 REFERENCE #3: O. Charran and Y.I. Lee. A Case of Dexmedetomidine (Precedex)-Induced Diabetes Insipidus in the Medical Intensive Care Unit. D49. CRITICAL CARE CASE REPORTS: NEUROCRITICAL CARE, SEDATION, AND DELIRIUM. May 1, 2019, A6634-A6634 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ahmad Abu Homoud No relevant relationships by Hira Akhlaq No relevant relationships by Ghadier Al Saoudi No relevant relationships by Albino Copca Alvarez No relevant relationships by Eric Costanzo No relevant relationships by Mohamed Elsawaf No relevant relationships by Farah Farhat No relevant relationships by Steven Imburgio No relevant relationships by Mihir Odak No relevant relationships by Claudia Ramirez No relevant relationships by Ndausung Udongwo No relevant relationships by Sydney ZoleziCopyright © 2023 American College of Chest Physicians
