T1  - Etomidate Drip for Treatment of Progressive Proximal Myopathy Due to Severe Cushing's Syndrome
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2023. Washington, DC United States.
A1  - Zehr E.
A1  - Agarwal N.
A1  - Rangarajan S.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Introduction: Etomidate is a commonly used induction agent for rapid sequence intubation (RSI). Its use remains controversial given debated increases in mortality due to adrenal suppression. Interestingly, this endocrinologic side effect has clinical utility. Here, we present a case of a patient with severe Cushing's syndrome (CS) that was managed with an etomidate drip. Case: A 54-year-old female with metastatic adrenocorticalcarcinoma presented with progressive difficulty in walking. Physical exam was notable for cushingoid features (with a round face, prominent dorsal fat pad, bilateral arm bruising) and strength of 3/5 hip flexion and 4/5 shoulder abduction bilaterally. Labs on presentation were remarkable for potassium of 2.1 mmol/L and a random serum cortisol of 87 mug/dL. A diagnosis of progressive proximal myopathy due to severe CS was made and she was admitted to the intensive care unit for an etomidate drip as a bridge to oral cortisol-reducing agents. The drip was titrated every two hours to reach a goal serum cortisol of 10-15 mug/dL. She was then successfully bridged to oral ketoconazole and metyrapone. Complications included hypokalemia and somnolence with the lowest documented RASS score of -1. Given the patient's poor overall prognosis, she ultimately transitioned to comfort care and passed away. Discussion(s): Etomidate is a rapid acting general anesthetic commonly used for RSI. Etomidate, through inhibition of 11beta-hydroxylase and aldosterone synthase activity, also causes adrenal suppression. Whether this results in worse outcomes in critically ill patients is debated. Four meta-analyses published between 2012 and 2021 discuss the effects of single bolus etomidate on mortality in critical illness, and demonstrate conflicting results. Etomidate was first described as treatment for hypercortisolism in 1986. A 2012 review of 43 patients suggests etomidate's higher efficacy in treating ACTH-independent CS compared to ACTH-dependent CS. Reported side effects include oversedation, thrombophlebitis when using peripheral access, and complications due to its formulation with propylene glycol. We modeled our protocol after that described by Carroll et al. In this case, we targeted a lower cortisol given the patient's hypoalbuminemia and titrated the drip every two hours as opposed to the recommended six. After the drip was stopped, her cortisol rebounded, prompting resumption while oral agents reached efficacy. During this course, the etomidate drip appeared to be tolerated without undue sedation, electrolyte abnormalities or hypoglycemia. An etomidate drip with bridge to oral agents is a safe, flexible, and efficacious treatment for severe hypercortisolism.
