T1  - Ketamine Utilization in Mechanically Ventilated Patients with COVID-19
T3  - Annual Meetings of the International Anesthesia Research Society and Society of Critical Care Anesthesiologists, IARS and SOCCA 2023. Denver, CO United States.
A1  - Garrigan E.
A1  - Krishnamoorthy V.
A1  - Royce-Nagel G.
A1  - Ohnuma T.
A1  - Fuller M.
A1  - Raghunathan K.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Introduction: COVID-19 has altered healthcare practices as patients with severe ARDS have been highly resistant to standard sedation regimens. Evidence in these critically ill patients has been conflicting or shown limited efficacy. Ketamine, most commonly used as an adjunct for pain management or as an induction agent, has unique properties that make it attractive for use in patients with severe COVID-19 infection. Ketamine possesses unique pharmacological characteristics such as bronchial smooth muscle relaxation and in vitro reduction of leukocyte activation during hypoxemia and sepsis; additionally it has been a useful adjunct in depression and status asthmaticus. The purpose of this study is to describe ketamine utilization patterns for early sedation among mechanically ventilated COVID-19 patients, and to examine hospital mortality and healthcare utilization in patients exposed to ketamine compared to unexposed patients. Method(s): We examined the records of 610 hospitals included in the Premier Healthcare Database for patients discharged between January 2020 and July 2021 with a diagnosis of COVID-19. Inclusion criteria included age greater than 18 years, mechanical ventilation within 5 days of admission and for at least 2 consecutive days, ketamine initiated within 2 days of intubation and continued for at least 2 consecutive days. Patients suffering from an acute MI or intracranial pathology and patients who were pregnant were excluded from our study. Using a propensity-matched analysis we analyzed the characteristics and in-hospital mortality of patients exposed to early ketamine and those that were not. Secondary outcomes investigated included: hospital length of stay, ICU length of stay, and total days on mechanical ventilation. Result(s): Our final cohort from the Premier Dataset included a total of 42954 patients. Our group of interest included 1423 patients who received ketamine per the inclusion criteria. We then used propensity score matching to obtain our ketamine and control groups of 1390 patients each. (Table 1) Due to the propensitymatching design of our study our groups were well balanced with regard to comorbidities and co-treatments, with the exception of the use of other sedatives within 2 days of IMV (Tables 3 and 4). While this did have a small effect size of .25, it was likely related to the high proportion of multimodal sedatives used in the ketamine group (99.9% vs 95.7%). Of note the Intraclass Correlation Coefficient for ketamine exposure was 0.39 indicating a potentially significant degree of variation in ketamine at the level of individual hospitals. Based on our propensity-matched analysis, there was a significant risk of in-hospital mortality and/or discharge to hospice associated with ketamine use (52.5% vs 45.9%, RR: 1.14 [1.06, 1.23]). Additionally, ECMO usage trended toward higher utilization in the ketamine group (6.5% vs 4.1%). However, our remaining secondary endpoints of days on mechanical ventilation, ICU length of stay and overall hospital length of stay were similar (Table 5) Conclusion(s): Ketamine was used more often in patients with severe COVID-19 symptoms and not necessarily because of comorbidities. The higher mortality in the ketamine population may be explained by a higher disease severity, as these patients had higher ECMO utilization. Our study design allowed us to exclude patients who received ketamine as rescue sedation after other regimens had failed. Despite this, as a retrospective data analysis, determining causation is challenging due to lack of randomization and risks for residual confounding. Additional limitations include that this is a claims database and not EHR where measurements occurred in days instead of hours. Additionally, Premier Dataset is inclusive primarily of southern hospitals and not necessarily representative of the nation. Lastly, there was wide variation in practice by hospital and this study design was unable to account for hospital culture and practice differences regarding ketamine with some centers having higher usage. There is large variation in ketamine use for mechanically-ventilated patients with COVID-19. However, research is necessary regarding its optimal use in patients with severe COVID-19.
