T1  - NEUROLEPTIC MALIGNANT SYNDROME AND SEROTONIN SYNDROME: A TALE OF TWO ETIOLOGIES OF HYPERPYREXIA
T3  - CHEST 2023 Annual Meeting. Honolulu United States.
A1  - Sinha Ray A.
A1  - Ghosh M.
A1  - Wittenberg Z.L.
A1  - Sahasranaman V.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - SESSION TITLE: Critical Care Case Report Posters 63 SESSION TYPE: Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/09/2023 12:00 pm - 12:45 pm INTRODUCTION: Fever is a common occurrence in ICU setting but, hyperpyrexia poses a unique diagnostic dilemma. Non-infectious differentials are broad including malignant hyperthermia (MH), neuroleptic malignant syndrome (NMS) and serotonin syndrome (SS). NMS is commonly linked to antipsychotics. SS is associated with serotonergic agents. With increasing use of combination therapy, sometimes it's difficult to differentiate between NMS and SS. Occasionally, drug-drug interaction could precipitate NMS. Here, we report a challenging case of hyperthermia where both an atypical antipsychotic and SNRI (Serotonin and Norepinephrine Reuptake Inhibitors) were on board. CASE PRESENTATION: A 64-year-old female with depression, hypertension, heart failure, COPD presented with respiratory distress. Initial labs were pertinent for acute kidney injury, elevated WBC and natriuretic peptide. Chest x-ray showed bibasilar infiltrate and vascular congestion. She was started on solumedrol, levofloxacin, IV diuretic for multifocal pneumonia and heart failure exacerbation. After failed trial of BiPAP, she got intubated with fentanyl, midazolam, etomidate and rocuronium as induction agents. Echo showed LVEF of 35% and RV dilation. About 72 hours after intubation, she abruptly developed a fever, which within few hours reached a maximum temperature of 44.5 degree C, and associated hemodynamic compromise requiring multiple vasopressors. Clonus or hyperreflexia were not appreciated on exam. Cooling catheter was placed for aggressive internal cooling. Home regimen included aripiprazole 5mg, venlafaxine 225mg, trazodone 100mg daily and few days prior to admission she was started on diphenhydramine for hives. Both SS and NMS were considered as differentials. She was becoming increasingly difficult to ventilate with multiple alarms for high peak and plateau pressures. Repeat chest X-ray ruled out pneumothorax. Chest wall rigidity was conceived explanation. Dantrolene was initiated; her hyperthermia resolved and inspiratory pressures improved, confirming our suspicion of NMS. DISCUSSION(S): Both NMS and SS results from drug-induced impairment of thermoregulation. Rigidity, bradyreflexia, autonomic instability are signs of NMS. SS, more common with SNRI than SSRI (Selective Serotonin Reuptake Inhibitors), usually presents within hours with hyperreflexia, clonus, mydriasis, nausea, vomiting and diarrhea. In our patient, potential interaction of diphenhydramine and aripiprazole may have precipitated NMS. Supportive measures including cessation of offending agents, correction of fluid-electrolyte imbalance, cooling, hemodynamic stabilization, agitation control with benzodiazepines are applicable to both entities. Fever higher than 41.1 degree C requires neuromuscular blockade following intubation, irrespective of etiology. Cyproheptadine, a serotonin antagonist, is mainstay therapy for SS. Dantrolene, Bromocriptine are pivotal in managing NMS. CONCLUSION(S): Detailed history and neurological examination are essential in differentiating NMS and SS. Timely diagnosis and management is crucial as NMS could be lethal. REFERENCE #1: Pileggi DJ, Cook AM. Neuroleptic Malignant Syndrome. Ann Pharmacother. 2016 Nov;50(11):973-981.doi:10.1177/1060028016657553. Epub 2016 Jul 19. REFERENCE #2: Boyer EW, Shannon M. The Serotonin Syndrome. N Engl J Med 2005; 352:1112-1120. DOI: 10.1056/NEJMra041867 REFERENCE #3: Cooper J, Duffull SB, Isbister GK. Predicting serotonin toxicity in serotonin reuptake inhibitor overdose. Clin Toxicol (Phila). 2023;61(1):22. Epub 2022 Nov 29 DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Meenakshi Ghosh No relevant relationships by Venketraman Sahasranaman No relevant relationships by Abhisekh Sinha Ray No relevant relationships by Zachariah WittenbergCopyright © 2023 American College of Chest Physicians
