T1  - Ketamine vs. Etomidate in Emergency Tracheal Intubation: Effect on 12-Month, Posttraumatic Stress Disorder Symptoms
T3  - Society for Academic Emergency Medicine Annual Meeting, SAEM 2023. Austin, TX United States.
A1  - Wollenman L.
A1  - Tipold A.
A1  - Semler M.W.
A1  - Casey J.D.
A1  - Lacy A.
A1  - Self W.H.
A1  - Kiehl A.L.
A1  - Bryant P.T.
A1  - Jackson J.
A1  - Ely E.W.
A1  - Han J.H.
Y1  - 2023//
N2  - Background and Objectives: One out of three intensive care unit survivors (ICU) who are mechanically ventilated develop post-traumatic stress disorder (PTSD). A single dose of ketamine has been shown to reduce symptoms in patients with chronic PTSD. We hypothesized that patients who received ketamine for emergent tracheal intubation would have fewer PTSD symptoms at 12 months than those who received etomidate. Method(s): This was a secondary analysis of the Cognitive Outcomes in Pragmatic Investigation of optimaL Oxygen Targets (CO-PILOT) trial, which was a cluster-randomized trial to determine if SpO2 targets were associated with 12-month cognition in patients who received invasive mechanical ventilation in the emergency department (ED) or ICU. Patients who received ketamine or etomidate for tracheal intubation and completed PTSD assessments at 12 months from 5/2020 to 7/2022 are included in this analysis. Induction agents for tracheal intubation were abstracted from the electronic health record. To characterize 12-month PTSD symptoms, trained neuropsychologist raters administered the PTSD Checklist for DSM-5 (PCL-5) by phone; scores range from 0 to 80, with higher scores indicating more severe PTSD symptoms. Proportional odds logistic regression was performed and was adjusted for age, race, sex, education, pre-illness depression or PTSD, comorbidities, severity of illness, sepsis, and location of intubation. Adjusted odds ratio (aOR) with their 95% confidence intervals (95% CI) are reported. Result(s): A total of 141 patients were included in this analysis; 89 (63%) received etomidate and 52 (37%) received ketamine for induction of tracheal intubation. Overall, the median (IQR) age was 56 (44, 65) years old, 62 (44%) were female, 23 (16%) were non-white race, 90 (63.8%) were intubated in the ED. The median (IQR) 12-month PCL-5 score was 14 (5, 27) and 7 (1, 18) for those who received etomidate and ketamine, respectively. Compared to etomidate, ketamine was associated with significantly lower PCL-5 scores at 12 months (aOR = 0.39, 95% CI: 0.20, 0.76). Conclusion(s): Compared with etomidate, induction with ketamine during emergency tracheal intubation was associated with a significantly lower burden of PTSD symptoms at 12 months. Because this is an observational study, these results may be affected by unmeasured confounders and a randomized trial is needed to confirm these findings.
