T1  - Effect of dexmedetomidine continuous infusion on sevoflurane requirements in patients undergoing neurosurgical procedures
A1  - Patel M.K.
A1  - Pradhan D.M.
A1  - Mishra A.K.
A1  - Ekka M.
A1  - Choudhury S.
A1  - Padhi N.
A1  - Patro G.S.P.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Background:It is a challenging task for anesthesiologists to maintain the hemodynamic stability in neurosurgical patients during intubation, intra-op period and during extubation. This Hospitalbased randomized prospectivecontrolled studyconducted to assess the effect of dexmedetomidine infusion on maintenance of perioperative hemodynamic parametersand to assess the reduction in the total requirement of sevoflurane. Method(s): 100 patients of ASA-I and II of both sexes and age group of 18-65 years scheduledfor elective or emergency neurosurgery under general anesthesia, with a GCS score of >=10 selected on the basis of inclusion criteria were assigned into two groups namely, Group SD [receiving Sevoflurane and Dexmedetomidine] and Group SP [receiving normal saline as placebo with Sevoflurane], with 50 patients in each. Heart rate(HR), systolic BP(SBP), diastolic BP(DBP), mean arterial pressure, EtCO2, SpO2 and inhaled concentration of Sevoflurane was monitored continuously and record maintained preoperatively followed by15 min interval up to the completion of surgery in both groups. Result(s):Age, sex, weight, height, ASA grade and duration of surgery for all patients in both the groups were compared. The decline in HR and rise in SBP, DBP and MAP in SD group after loading dose of dexmedetomidine was statistically significant. The decline in HR, SBP, DBP, MAP during intraoperative period of dexmedetomidine group is more comparable than control group. The total amount of sevoflurane used in dexmedetomidine group was also less compared to control group.Dexmedetomidine significantly reduces the time forextubation and recovery as compared to placebo group. Conclusion(s):Dexmedetomidine continuous infusion provided betterhemodynamicstabilityasan adjuvant to sevoflurane during general anesthesia for neurosurgical procedures, reduces intubation and recovery time, and also reduces the sevoflurane consumption.Copyright © 2022, Anka Publishers. All rights reserved.
