T1  - Inhaled Sevoflurane Sedation vs Propofol in Intubated Patients with Severe Exacerbation of COPD: A Pilot Randomised Controlled Study
T3  - International Conferenceof the American Thoracic Society, ATS 2022. San Francisco, CA United States.
A1  - Jung B.
A1  - Garnier F.
A1  - Sibari M.
A1  - Bourdin A.
A1  - Amalric M.
A1  - Brunot V.
A1  - Klouche K.
A1  - Soler M.
A1  - Molinari N.
A1  - Matecki S.
Y1  - 2022//
N2  - Introduction: Patients with COPD exacerbation show expiratory airflow limitation, increased airway resistance, gas trapping, dynamic hyperinflation and intrinsic positive end-expiratory pressure. About 25% of critically ill patients with exacerbation of COPD need invasive mechanical ventilation (MV) (1). Although sevoflurane is a volatile anesthetic with reported bronchodilatation properties (2), its effect on expiratory airflow in severe exacerbation of critically ill COPD patients needing MV is unknown. The purpose of this study was to compare inhaled sevoflurane vs intravenous propofol on respiratory mechanics in critically ill COPD patients needing MV. Method(s): Single center, openlabel, randomized clinical trial (NCT03460015; IRB2017002504-27). Intubated patients with severe exacerbation of COPD and expected duration of MV of at least 48h with no contra-indications of propofol or sevoflurane were randomized in two groups (propofol or sevoflurane (Anaconda, Sedana Medical, Danderyd, Sweden)) after next of kin then patients consent. MV settings, clinical sedation targets and medical treatments were standardised and identical in the two groups. Expiratory fraction of sevoflurane target was 1-1.5%. The following parameters were measured or calculated upon inclusion and after 30min,4h,8h,12h,24h,36h,48h and every 24h until extubation: vitals, peak pressure, pressure at zero flow (P1), plateau pressure after 5s of zero flow, endexpiratory pressure (administered/total), total airway (Raw) and interrupter resistances (3). Gas trapping was measured using the end expiratory pause technique provided by the ventilator.The primary endpoint was the total airway resistance kinetic from H0 to H24 between the two groups. Expecting a 7+/-5cmH2O/l/s total airway resistance difference between the two groups after 24h of treatment, we planned to enroll 22 patients. Result(s): The study was stopped for futility after having enrolled 16 patients. The main results are presented in table 1. There was no difference in any endpoints between the two groups at H24. However, using a mixed model taking into account repeated measures and after adjustment on the baseline data, propofol but not sevoflurane was associated with a decrease in Raw by 0.03cmH2O/l/s by H24, p=0.04. Conclusion(s): Deep sedation with sevoflurane is feasible and safe in patients with severe exacerbation of COPD. We did not observe any statistically significant or clinically relevant effect of sevoflurane on respiratory mechanics parameters in these patients.1. Demoule A et-al Intensive Care Med. 2020- Dec;46(12):2436-49.2. Volta CA et-al, Anesth Analg. 2005-Feb;100(2):348-53.3. Guerin C et- Richard J-C, Intensive Care Med. 2007-May 24;33(6):1046-9.
