T1  - Evaluating the analgesic effect of ketamine in mechanically ventilated patients
T3  - 50th Critical Care Congress. Virtual.
A1  - Zada I.
A1  - Alzyood L.
A1  - Paniagua J.
A1  - Saldana R.V.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - INTRODUCTION: Pain management is critical for mechanically ventilated patients. Due to the robust data evaluating their use, opioids are the analgesics most commonly utilized. However, increased opioid use can lead to potential opioid tolerance, addiction, and undesirable side effects. Ketamine has been proven to be noninferior to fentanyl in multiple clinical trials; however, none of these studies were conducted in intubated patients in the Intensive Care Unit (ICU). METHOD(S): This was a retrospective cohort study evaluating adult patients aged 18 years and older, admitted to a Medical Intensive Care Unit (MICU), and requiring mechanical ventilation, who received either a ketamine or fentanyl infusion for analgesia. The primary outcome was pain measured by the Critical Care Pain Observation Tool (CPOT). Secondary outcomes included vasopressor requirements, sedative requirements, adverse effects, and duration of analgesia. Outcomes were evaluated by converting doses to the morphine equivalent intervals of 0.03-0.08 mg/kg/hr, 0.09-0.16 mg/kg/hr, and >=0.17 mg/kg/hr in both groups. RESULT(S): 53 patients (26 ketamine, 27 fentanyl) were included in the study with no significant differences seen in baseline characteristics. No significant difference was noted in the median CPOT scores throughout all dosing intervals of ketamine and fentanyl: 1 versus 1 (p=1.00), 1 versus 0.5 (p=0.6985), and 1 versus 0 (p=0.4226), respectively. Similarly, there were no significant differences observed in the secondary outcomes aside from a higher Confusion Assessment Method for the ICU (CAM-ICU) score in the ketamine group. This did not translate to increased use of antipsychotics and can likely be attributed to the limited documentation of CAM-ICU scores. CONCLUSION(S): Based on results of this study, ketamine's analgesic effect is comparable to that of fentanyl. Further randomized controlled trials are needed to validate results of this study and to assess long-term sequelae of opioid and ketamine treatment.
