T1  - PREDICTORS OF RE-INTUBATION AMONG PATIENTS WITH COVID-19
T3  - CHEST 2021 Annual Meeting. Virtual, Online.
A1  - Halaseh R.
A1  - Masri I.
A1  - Drescher G.
A1  - Al Ahmad M.
A1  - Al-deen Mohammad S.
A1  - Arar T.
A1  - Chan C.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - TOPIC: Critical Care TYPE: Original Investigations PURPOSE: Severe acute respiratory failure requiring intubation can be a devastating consequence of COVID-19 infection resulting in significant mortality. In general ICU populations, extubation failure occurs in 10-20% of patients and is associated with mortality rates from 25-50%. Reintubation rates and subsequent clinical outcomes are not well documented in COVID-19 pneumonia. METHOD(S): We conducted a single-center, retrospective study in a tertiary care hospital of all intubated COVID-19 patients who passed a spontaneous breathing trial from March 2020-March 2021. Patients were included if they were reintubated within 72 h of extubation and excluded if extubated for comfort care. Our primary outcome was reintubation rate; secondary outcomes included variables associated with reintubation and all-cause mortality. Data collected included baseline demographics, comorbid conditions, laboratory values and sedative medications used 24-48h prior to a planned extubation, hospital and ICU length of stay (LOS), and mortality. Preliminary analyses were conducted using chi-square testing, Student's t test, and Wilcoxon rank-sum test, as appropriate. Multivariate logistic regression models were created to predict variables associated with reintubation and mortality. A P<=0.10 was used to determine variables to be included in regression analysis. RESULT(S): The cohort consisted of 108 patients (mean age: 56.6+/-14.9 male: 53%); 36% required reintubation. There were no differences for age, sex, race, BMI, or major co-morbidities including diabetes, hypertension, or underlying pulmonary, cardiovascular, or chronic kidney disease between those who did and did not require reintubation. Lab values were similar between groups (ESR, CRP, Troponin, CPK, D-dimer, LDH; all P >=.10). Univariate variables with a P<=0.10 included in the multivariate analysis included age, chronic kidney disease, fentanyl, propofol, ketamine, and midazolam. Treatment for COVID-19 was similar between patients requiring reintubation vs. those who did not: remdesivir (26% vs. 29%; P=.71), dexamethasone (62% vs. 51%; P=.27), and tocilizumab (13% vs. 17%; P=.53), respectively. There was significantly greater ketamine (13% vs. 3%; P=.044), fentanyl (77% vs. 45%; P=.001), and midazolam (67% vs. 14%; P<.0001) use 24-48 h prior to extubation in those requiring reintubation. LOS was higher in those reintubated: hospital (31.5 d vs. 24.7 d; P=.02) and ICU (23.9 vs. 13.8 d; P<.001) LOS. Morality rate was higher among those reintubated (41% vs. 3%; P<.0001). On multivariate analysis age (OR 1.03 [CI 1.002-1.069]; P=.039) and midazolam use prior to extubation (OR 13.8 [CI 5.1-37.8]; P<.0001) were independently associated with reintubation. Logistic regression for mortality found male sex (OR.18 [CI.04-.76]; P=.02), diabetes (OR 4.01 [CI 1.07-15.03]; P=.039), and reintubation (OR 26.2 [CI 5.1-133.9]; P<.0001) independently associated with death. CONCLUSION(S): COVID-19 patients experienced higher reintubation rates compared to general ICU populations. Age and midazolam use were associated with reintubation. Reintubated patients had higher hospital and ICU LOS, as well as mortality. Male sex, comorbid diabetes and reintubation were independently associated with death among COVID-19 patients in this cohort. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: COVID-19 patients are at higher risk for reintubation which may be associated with an increased risk for death. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Mamoon Al Ahmad, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Tareq Arar, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Chee Chan, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Gail Drescher, source=Web Response, value=Consulting fee Removed 04/30/2021 by Gail Drescher, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Gail Drescher, source=Web Response, value=Salary Removed 04/30/2021 by Gail Drescher, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Ramez Halaseh, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Ihab Masri, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Saad Al-deen Mohammad, source=Web ResponseCopyright © 2021 American College of Chest Physicians
