T1  - A Case of Status Asthmaticus Refractory to Conventional Treatment Strategies
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2021. Virtual.
A1  - Wasio A.
A1  - Sanchez Fernandez L.L.
A1  - Saintil M.
A1  - Anis K.
A1  - Singh H.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - Introduction:Bronchial asthma is one of the most common chronic conditions among young adults affecting millions of people worldwide. Airway inflammation causing airway edema as well as smooth muscle hyper-reactivity leads to bronchial obstruction and hyperinflation posing difficulties for intensivist to mechanically ventilate those patients. [1]Case presentation:A 23-year-old male with past medical history of asthma with multiple prior intubations, was admitted from ER with shortness of breath secondary to an acute exacerbation in setting of medication non-compliance, poly-substance use and vaping. He was in moderate distress, able to speak in full sentences initially. He was tachycardic (104), with normal respiratory rate. Physical examination showed decreased breath sounds with diffuse wheezing. Labs showed WBC18.1, hemoglobin 16.3, hematocrit 47.9 and eosinophils 0.7. The patient's initial ABG showed pH 7.04, PCO2 115, PO2 292 and HCO3 19. Chest X ray was normal. Influenza PCR negative. Conventional asthma treatment (Albuterol nebulization & steroids) were administered with temporary relief, then he started feeling more short of breath and had increased work of breathing. He was initially placed on BiPAP with no improvement. He was intubated in ER, but remained very difficult to ventilate. The patient was disconnected from the ventilator was ambu bagged. Multi-pharmacological interventions including multiple nebulizer treatments, systemic steroids, magnesium sulfate, IV ketamine, Epinephrine infusion, racemic epinephrine nebulizations, heliox and lidocaine 1% airway flush were unsuccessful. The patient was taken to OR for trial of an inhalational anesthetic gas, sevoflurane, which was delivered via the ventilating circuit through the conventional mode of variable flow bypass which resulted in relief of bronchospasm and ease of ventilation. The patient's acidosis significantly improved along with his clinical picture and was eventually discharged to a rehab facility. Conclusion(s):We present a case of status asthmaticus in a young adult with multiple prior intubations and poorly controlled asthma who did not respond to conventional therapy requiring an inhalational anesthetic, sevoflurane, to relieve bronchospasm. There are currently no specific guidelines regarding the use of inhalational anesthetics in severe status asthamticus. It's use should be considered as a rescue therapy in severe status asthmaticus refractory to conventional treatment due to its effect of decreasing airway resistance, intrinsic PEEP and rapid bronchodilataion. A multidisciplinary team approach between ICU team, anesthesia and respiratory therapy is required to administer this rescue therapy.
