T1  - PROPOFOL-RELATED INFUSION SYNDROME IN A PATIENT WITH ARDS SECONDARY TO THE NOVEL COVID-19
T3  - CHEST 2021 Annual Meeting. Virtual, Online.
A1  - Freeman H.
A1  - Moulton B.
Y1  - 2021//
N2  - TOPIC: Critical Care TYPE: Medical Student/Resident Case Reports INTRODUCTION: Propofol-related infusion syndrome (PRIS) is a rare and often fatal complication characterized by metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, lipemia, renal failure, hepatomegaly, bradycardia and cardiovascular collapse.[1] Its mechanism of action is poorly understood, but is typically associated with very large doses of propofol (>4 mg/kg/hr or >67 mcg/kg/min) as well as prolonged use (>48 hrs).[2] CASE PRESENTATION: A 24 year-old male with morbid obesity (BMI 67) was admitted to the ICU with severe ARDS secondary to COVID-19 pneumonia. He was intubated and treated with remdesivir and dexamethasone. His condition worsened despite lung-protective strategies and a paralysis trial, so he was placed on VV-ECMO for further support. There was initially great difficulty oxygenating the patient, necessitating high ECMO pump flows and delta pressures. This resulted in a large level of hemolysis that led to anuric renal failure from pigment-induced nephropathy, requiring initiation of CRRT. His oxygenation improved after several rounds of proning. However, he required large quantities of sedation with propofol at a rate between 50-60 mcg/kg/min in order to accomplish this. Triglyceride levels remained mildly elevated, but stable. While his overall condition improved over several days, he developed a hyperleukocytosis during this time (30->71) that continued to worsen despite treatment with broad spectrum antibiotics. Over a period of 12 hours, the patient became bradycardic and his blood pressure declined, requiring initiation of pressor support. Repeat labs were notable for WBC 114, lactate 14.8, CK 4700, triglycerides 962 and worsening metabolic acidosis (pH 7.19, bicarb 13) despite continued CRRT. The patient abruptly suffered cardiac arrest and cardiopulmonary resuscitative efforts were unfortunately unsuccessful. DISCUSSION(S): The patient's initial marker for a developing secondary process was his hyperleukocytosis, which was felt to be too high for an infectious etiology or secondary to steroids and too rapid for a hematologic malignancy. Generalized marrow stimulation in the setting of recent hemolysis was also considered. After multidisciplinary discussion, further consideration was given to PRIS as the etiology for his rapid decline. He was administered high doses of propofol for an extended period of time and his morbid obesity placed him at higher risk for toxic accumulation given that propofol is highly lipophilic with a large volume of distribution. Furthermore, additional proposed risk factors include young age, critical illness and concomitant catecholamine or steroid use, [3] all of which were present in this case. CONCLUSION(S): Propofol-related infusion syndrome is an important phenomenon to be aware of in the ICU setting. It carries a high mortality rate and treatment is only supportive. As such, alternative sedative therapies should be considered when high dose or prolonged sedation is needed. REFERENCE #1: Fong JJ, Sylvia L, Ruthazer R, et al. Predictors of mortality in patients with suspected propofol infusion syndrome. Crit Care Med 2008; 36:2281. REFERENCE #2: Hemphill S, McMenamin L, Bellamy MC, Hopkins PM. Propofol infusion syndrome: a structured literature review and analysis of published case reports. Br J Anaesth 2019; 122:448. REFERENCE #3: Diedrich DA, Brown DR. Analytic reviews: propofol infusion syndrome in the ICU. J Intensive Care Med 2011; 26:59. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Howard Freeman, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Bart Moulton, source=Web ResponseCopyright © 2021 American College of Chest Physicians
