T1  - Close the bag: a project to methodically improve the quality of the emergency airway bag
T3  - World Airway Management Meeting. Amsterdam Netherlands.
A1  - Samuels J.
A1  - Like B.
A1  - Mack P.
A1  - Malhotra V.
A1  - Spiegel M.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - Emergency anesthesia airway bags (EAABs) are ubiquitous in tertiary medical centers yet there is little in the literature that discusses them.1,2 Our institution's EAAB was often overfilled, yet inconsistently equipped, without clear guidelines as to how it should be stocked. We developed a new standardized checklist of contents in order to consistently maintain adequate par levels of medications and essential airway supplies. Additionally, we researched various styles of EAABs to maximize functionality and ease of transport. Data was collected at NewYork-Presbyterian/Weill Cornell Medical Center from September through November 2018 via surveys of drugs and airway equipment used during emergency intubations, equipment preferences of anesthesiology attendings and residents, and random checks of our EAAB. Data was collected regarding supplies available in code carts and intubation boxes available throughout the hospital. Mathematical models and discussions with senior faculty and pharmacists were then used to determine a supplies checklist for our EAAB. All ethical guidelines of our institution were followed. Random checks of equipment in our EAAB demonstrated inconsistently stocked airway supplies and medications, which translated to significant variability in the weight of the bag (2.5-3.4kg). During emergent intubations, McGrath 3 was the favored laryngoscope (>85%) along with size #8.0 endotracheal tubes (>50%), while rocuronium, succinylcholine, etomidate, propofol and phenylephrine were the most frequently used drugs. Successive airway emergencies occurred frequently, which at times led to a lack of necessary supplies and/or medications. Our institution's new EAAB checklist (Figure 1) standardizes the par levels of airway equipment and medications to be stocked in the EAAB. This final list was guided by physician survey responses and the data documenting supplies and medications utilized during intubations. In addition, this new EAAB supply checklist includes sufficient supplies/medications for successive airway emergencies. Post-implementation data and physician surveys will be obtained to assess physician satisfaction and document improvement in the availability of appropriate supplies and medications. References [1] Apfelbaum JL, Hagberg CA, Caplan RA, et al. Anesthesiology 2013; 118: 251-70 [2] Frerk C, Mitchell VS, McNarry AF, et al. Br J Anaesth 2015; 115: 827-48 [Formula presented]Copyright © 2020
