T1  - INFLUENZA B DOES NOT EXPLAIN THIS: A CASE REPORT OF AN UNUSUALLY DIFFICULT EXTUBATION
T3  - CHEST 2020 Annual Meeting. Virtual, Online.
A1  - Houle M.
A1  - Sjulin T.
A1  - Crawley P.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - SESSION TITLE: Fellows Procedures Posters SESSION TYPE: Fellow Case Report Posters PRESENTED ON: October 18-21, 2020 INTRODUCTION: Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage has an extensive differential diagnosis. This case emphasized that accurate history taking, and careful observation can help narrow the differential and guide management. CASE PRESENTATION: A 36 year old physically fit military First Sergeant presented to his local emergency department complaining of 9 days of severe joint and lower back pain associated with shortness of breath after vacationing in Hawaii. In extremis, intubation was his only option. His course was immediately complicated by hemoptysis and rapidly worsening respiratory failure. He tested positive for influenza B and developed fulminant ARDS. Anticipating the need for ECMO he was transferred to our facility but the next day he was on minimal ventilator settings and his CXR had dramatically. He became agitated as the sedation was decreased and decompensated requiring a bag valve mask to maintain adequate oxygenation followed by repeat paralysis, and high PEEP ventilation. This episode was caused by biting down on the endotracheal tube, writhing in discomfort and guppy breathing. His labs showed a worsening anemia and a bronchoalveolar lavage returned increasing bloody aliquots. Given the recent vacation, he was treated empirically for leptospirosis and serologic testing later confirmed this diagnosis. Our clinical impression was that the patient likely had diffuse pulmonary capillaritis in the setting of both influenza B and leptospirosis and subsequent pulmonary hemorrhage from negative pressure generated during his initial intubation and subsequently with lightening of his sedation. His extubation was complicated twice more by similar episodes. The patient was extubated successfully after using a modified extubation strategy requiring continuous infusion of ketamine 50 mcg/kg/min plus olanzapine 10mg via oral gastric tube 20 minutes prior to extubation and dexmedetomidine infusion of 2.0 mcg/kg/h. The patient was discharged from the hospital on room air and has made a full recovery. DISCUSSION(S): This case shows how increased negative intrathoracic pressure in the setting of a lung infection can contribute to DAH. For this patient, intubation was a necessary evil, complicated by agitated delirium-caused alveolar hemorrhage which cycled back to continued intubation. Difficult extubations due to agitations are common enough to be described both in peer-reviewed studies(2) and in Free Access Online Meducation(3) however they do not have a guideline approach. Our approach was safe and effective. CONCLUSION(S): Mechanically induced diffuse alveolar hemorrhage when waking up from sedation in the setting of leptospirosis and influenza B has never been described before. Difficult extubations due to agitation do not have a guideline approach and our method was safe and effective. Reference #1: Luks, Andrew M., Sambasiva Lakshminarayanan, and Jan V. Hirschmann. "Leptospirosis presenting as diffuse alveolar hemorrhage: case report and literature review." Chest 123.2 (2003): 639-643. Reference #2: Reade, Michael C., et al. "Effect of dexmedetomidine added to standard care on ventilator-free time in patients with agitated delirium: a randomized clinical trial." Jama 315.14 (2016): 1460-1468. Reference #3: Farkas, J. "PulmCrit Wee- Extubating the agitated patient: dexmedetomidine vs. cowboy-style?" April 20, 2016. https://emcrit.org/pulmcrit/extubating-agitated-patient-dexmedetomidine/ DISCLOSURES: no disclosure on file for Peter Crawley; No relevant relationships by Mateo Houle, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Tyson Sjulin, source=Web ResponseCopyright © 2020 American College of Chest Physicians
