T1  - Steroid-responsive encephalopathy associated with autoimmune thyroiditis (hashimoto's encephalopathy) complicated by status epilepticus successfully treated with therapeutic plasma exchange
T3  - Annual Meeting of the American Society for Apheresis, ASFA 2020. Virtual.
A1  - Torrez M.
A1  - Crookston K.
A1  - Griggs J.
A1  - Rosenbaum L.
A1  - Reyes C.
A1  - Prizzi M.
A1  - Raval J.S.
Y1  - 2020//
N2  - Purpose: Steroid-responsive encephalopathy associated with autoimmune thyroiditis (SREAT), i.e. Hashimoto's encephalopathy, is a rare syndrome characterized by encephalopathy of unknown etiology in the absence of alternative diagnoses and elevated anti-thyroid antibody titers. Previously reported treatments include immunosuppressants (typically corticosteroids) and therapeutic plasma exchange (TPE), the latter being a category II indication with grade 2c recommendation for use in this syndrome. We present a case report of a patient with SREAT successfully treated with TPE. Method(s): The medical record was queried for clinical and laboratory information. Result(s): The patient is a 70-year-old female with extensive medical history including chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, focal seizures complicated by epilepsia partialis continua, recurrent encephalopathy, hypothyroidism, bipolar disorder, type II diabetes, and SREAT associated with focal seizures responsive to high-dose corticosteroids. After discharge from hospital one month previously for SREAT-associated seizures and outpatient treatment with corticosteroid therapy, she was admitted for acute sub-massive pulmonary embolism and seizures. In addition to continuing oral corticosteroids, she also received heparin, levothyroxine, and multiple anti-epileptics. On hospital day 2, she had an increased oxygen requirement and was placed on non-invasive ventilation support; there was also concern for possible acute psychosis secondary to chronic corticosteroid use. She continued to have agitation and anxiety with suspected delirium. On day 13, the patient's seizure activity worsened, and lorazepam and phenytoin were added to her anti-epileptic regimen. She then went into status epilepticus and was transferred to ICU. On day 18, she was intubated and received midazolam as well as another course of high-dose corticosteroids. While her encephalopathy was noted to have improved slightly, she had continued seizure activity and received ketamine. On day 41, a tracheostomy tube was placed and an additional course of high-dose corticosteroids was administered. Despite these measures, she was not responding to pharmacotherapies as she had previously, and so a trial of 7 TPE procedures was initiated on day 52. A nontunneled double-lumen central venous catheter was used for vascular access, 1.0 plasma volumes were processed with each TPE, and albumin was used as replacement fluid. At TPE initiation, though the patient's seizures were controlled, she remained nonverbal, altered, had abnormal facial grimacing, and neither followed commands nor responded to questions. She spontaneously opened her eyes and moved extremities. After TPE #3, she was slightly more alert, attempted to communicate with simple sentences, and was following simplecommands. By day 61, she was transferred out of ICU. After TPE #6, communication improved further and she was oriented to person, place, and situation. On day 69, a tracheostomy capping trial was initiated, and this was ultimately removed on day 70. Due to her significant improvement with 7 TPE procedures, her Neurology team proceeded with apheresis port placement and plans for long-term TPE 1-2 times per week for the next 6 months while deliberating additional immunosuppression regimens. Conclusion(s): TPE was deemed to be an effective therapy for this patient with SREAT. The use of TPE as a longterm treatment for this patient may provide further insights into the impacts of apheresis therapy in this syndrome.
