T1  - Does early use of dexmedetomidine impact ICU outcomes in patients with alcohol withdrawal?
T3  - 48th Critical Care Congress of the Society of Critical Care Medicine, SCCM 2019. San Diego, CA United States.
A1  - Farina N.
A1  - Alaniz C.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Learning Objectives: Alcohol withdrawal remains challenging to treat. Recent literature has shown that dexmedetomidine (DEX) substantially reduces benzodiazepine requirements in patients suffering from withdrawal. However, the data does not note a corresponding reduction in ICU or inpatient length of stay. The goal of this study was to determine if early DEX use after ICU admission was associated with improved outcomes in patients with alcohol withdrawal. Method(s):Patients aged 18 years or greater who were treated for alcohol withdrawal while in an ICU between January 2014 and January 2018 were included. Patients who received less than 5 mg of oral lorazepam equivalents total were excluded. Patients who received DEX within 72 hours of ICU admission were compared to those who either did not receive DEX or received DEX over 72 after ICU admission. Data collected included baseline characteristics, length of stay, duration of mechanical ventilation, and medications administered. Groups were compared using descriptive statistics, Student's T test, Mann-Whitney U test, and Chi Square test. Result(s): Overall, 154 patients were included; 64 in the early DEX group and 90 in the late or no DEX group. Patients in the early DEX group received greater amounts of benzodiazepines (20.3 vs. 9.7 mg per day oral lorazepam equivalents, p<0.001). However, propofol was used less often in patients who received early DEX (65% vs.82%, p=0.03). Patients who received early DEX were less likely to be intubated during ICU stay (76.6% vs. 92.2%, p=0.009). However, there was no difference in days alive and ventilator free (86 vs. 86 days, p=0.15), ICU length of stay (7.3 vs. 7.8 days, p=0.94), or inpatient length of stay (15.7 vs. 21.0 days, p=0.164). Rates of sepsis, septic shock, and ARDS were similar between groups. Those who received early DEX were more likely to be transferred to the ICU for treatment of withdrawal (37.5% vs. 12.2%, p<0.001). Conclusion(s): In this study, use of DEX within 72 hours of ICU admission did not significantly impact length of stay. DEX did not demonstrate benzodiazepine-sparing properties; however, DEX was likely used in patients with more severe withdrawal. It may be prudent to trial other adjunctive therapies instead of or in addition to DEX early in treatment for alcohol withdrawal.
