T1  - THE EFFECTS OF PROPOFOL ON VASOPRESSOR REQUIREMENTS BASED ON UNDERLYING CARDIAC STATUS IN PATIENTS WITH VASODILATORY SHOCK
T3  - CHEST 2019 Annual Meeting. New Orleans United States.
A1  - Garimella B.
A1  - Elnadoury O.
A1  - Khorolsky C.
A1  - Iskandir C.
A1  - Mercado J.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - SESSION TITLE: Tuesday Abstract Posters SESSION TYPE: Original Investigation Posters PRESENTED ON: 10/22/2019 01:00 PM - 02:00 PM PURPOSE: Despite its favorable pharmacokinetic profile for the purposes of continuous sedation, propofol carries a precaution with regards to use in patients with underlying cardiac dysfunction, such that patients with an ejection fraction (EF) METHODS: This is a multicenter, retrospective chart review of critically ill adult patients admitted to intensive care units (ICU) from January 2015 to October 2018. Inclusion criteria were an ICD-10 diagnosis of hemorrhagic or septic shock, propofol use for continuous sedation within 48 hours of mechanical ventilation, and a formal echocardiogram during admission. Exclusion criteria included the primary diagnosis of cardiogenic shock, propofol administration for rapid sequence intubation, out-of-hospital intubation, hypertriglyceridemia, or concomitant administration of dexmedetomidine. The primary endpoint was vasopressor requirement, in norepinephrine-equivalents, required to maintain hemodynamic targets. Secondary endpoints included the absolute change in mean arterial pressure (MAP), maximum rates, duration and number of vasopressors required. Additionally, hospital and ICU lengths of stay, and in-hospital mortality were evaluated. RESULT(S): A total of 22 patients were analyzed (13 in the pEF group and 9 in the rEF group). There was no statistically significant difference in vasopressor requirements between the pEF and rEF groups (52.5 mcg/min versus 38.1 mcg/min, P =0.596). Median absolute change in MAP was not significantly different between the pEF and rEF groups (-16 vs -2, p = 0.332). Patients in both groups required a median of two vasopressors to sustain hemodynamics. All patients received norepinephrine and there was no difference in maximum rates or duration of therapy. Of the 9 patients who received vasopressin, the duration of therapy in the rEF group was numerically higher (15.5 vs 55.9 hours) but did not reach statistical significance (p=0.084). There was also no difference in length of stay or in-hospital mortality. CONCLUSION(S): Based on our findings, the underlying cardiac status does not affect hemodynamic response to propofol infusion. A larger trial is warranted to confirm these findings. CLINICAL IMPLICATIONS: Our study illustrates the probable safety of propofol infusion in vasodilatory shock regardless of cardiac status. DISCLOSURES: No relevant relationships by Ola Elnadoury, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Bhaskara Garimella, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Carina Iskandir, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Ciril Khorolsky, source=Web Response No relevant relationships by Jorge Mercado, source=Web ResponseCopyright © 2019 American College of Chest Physicians
