T1  - Laryngeal mucosal tear and creation of false passage after failed difficult intubation
T3  - Trainee Conference 2019. Telford United Kingdom.
A1  - Mohamed M.
A1  - Srinivasan C.
A1  - Motawea I.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Intubation of the trachea can result in direct injury to the larynx. This can occur either from traumatic passage of a tracheal tube on intubation and extubation or from intra-operative pressure and irritation from the rigid portion of the tube. We present a female patient presenting for emergency laparatomy, in whom submucosal dissection of the larynx occurred during attempted trials of difficult intubation. Previous similar reports of this complication are not common after reviewing the literature. Description A 65-year-old female patient presented to our hospital with a strangulated femoral hernia after a week of abdominal pain and vomiting. Pre-operative physical examination revealed well-controlled hypertension on ramipril, dyslipidaemia on statins and hypothyroidism on levothyroxine. She had a history of two uneventful general anaesthetics under laryngeal masks for knee arthroscopies. On the day of the surgery, she was pre-assessed by the senior registrar on-call who documented a low Mallampati score (class I) and thyromental distance of 6 cm, one missing tooth and normal neck movement. Generally, she was physically active, living alone and her body mass index was 28. When she presented for surgery, she was attached to routine monitoring according to Association of Anaesthetists' guidelines. Anaesthesia was induced with rapid sequence induction and after pre-oxygenation and using 100 lg fentanyl, 200 mg propofol, 70 mg rocuronium; a trial of intubation was performed after 60 s by the senior registrar, which was unsuccessful. Difficult Airway Society (DAS) guidelines were followed, with another trial after changing the position of the patient to the sniffing position and release of cricoid pressure; however, it failed with Cormack- Lehane view of 3. Mask ventilation was re-attempted and the consultant took over and attempted intubation thrice, each time getting a grade III view and forcing a bougie, which seemed to appear on the left side of the neck on superficial palpation. As the intubation failed, the plan was to attempt using an i-gel and to proceed with surgery as the mask ventilation was intact throughout. The initial impression was that there was a subglottic lesion hindering the passage of the bougie; consequently, the Ear, Nose and Throat (ENT) consultant on-call was contacted, who came and performed nasendoscopy, which revealed a mucosal tear at the level of the glottis and subglottic region producing approximately 8 cm of false passage and oedema of the cords. The patient completed her surgery with i-gel and extubated in the recovery uneventfully. Hoarseness of the voice was noted postoperatively and persisted for a week thereafter.
