T1  - "Saved by sevo"-sevoflurane in refractory adult status asthmaticus
T3  - 2019 International Conference of the American Thoracic Society , ATS 2019. Dallas, TX United States.
A1  - Littlefield L.
A1  - Kagunye S.
A1  - Jo L.
A1  - Balasubramanian V.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Inhaled volatile anesthetics have been used in children to treat status Asthmaticus, but their role in adults is described only in a handful of case reports. Refractory status asthmaticus (RSA) can pose a unique challenge in the ICU and carries significant morbidity and mortality. We present a case of RSA in which Inhalational anesthetic agent Sevoflurane (SF) was effective in reducing Bronchospasm. 45-year-old African-American Female, marijuana smoker, with a history of asthma presented with 3 days of worsening dyspnea and cough not relieved by rescue inhaler. She was found to be hypertensive, tachypneic, and tachycardic. Chest auscultation revealed a "silent chest" with evidence of poor ventilation. She was intubated for hypercapnic respiratory failure. Worsening hypercapnic acidosis and elevated peak pressures prompted initiation of deep sedation followed by neuro-muscular blockade (NMB). Despite conventional bronchodilator treatments, corticosteroids, maximal sedation, NMB and other interventions such as Ketamine, patient continued to demonstrate severe bronchospasm with increasing peak pressures (36-42), tachycardia and worsening hypercapnic acidemia in ICU Day 4. A trial of inhalational anesthetic SF was considered based upon evidence from few case reports. SF inhalation was initiated, arterial blood gasses shown in figure 1. Neuromuscular blockade was discontinued within 4 hours of cessation of SF with continued improvement in ventilation and sustained reduction in peak (21) and plateau pressures (17). Acute asthma exacerbation mostly responds to beta2- adrenergic agonist and corticosteroids. Acute severe asthma can lead to respiratory failure but will often respond to sedation and NMB. Status asthmaticus is an acute, intractable asthma attack that may be refractory to standard therapy. This case illustrates the need for consideration of non-conventional therapy. The use of inhaled anesthetics for asthma dates to the 1930's. SF, an inhalational volatile anesthetic agent has significant Bronchodilator properties and prior studies have demonstrated superior bronchodilation compared to isoflurane or halothane. Refractory Status Asthmaticus may be associated with a serious prognosis and mortality risk. Sevoflurane may have a therapeutic role in select cases which may help avert a tragic outcome. Given the accumulating collective experiences, a formal study with this intervention may need to be strongly considered in future (Figure Presented) .
