T1  - Hemodynamic effects of ketamine versus etomidate during rapid sequence intubation in an ED
T3  - 48th Critical Care Congress of the Society of Critical Care Medicine, SCCM 2019. San Diego, CA United States.
A1  - Nakajima S.
A1  - Taylor K.
A1  - Hall Zimmerman L.
A1  - Collopy K.
A1  - Fales C.
A1  - Powers W.
Y1  - 2019//
N2  - Learning Objectives: Rapid sequence intubation (RSI) is often required for hemodynamically unstable patients; therefore, utilizing an induction agent with neutral hemodynamic effects is imperative. Ketamine and etomidate are indicated for induction during RSI. Ketamine may be preferred for hemodynamically unstable patients given its theoretically positive hemodynamic profile, however, this effect has not been evaluated in a prospective study. The objective of this study is to determine if there is a difference in hemodynamics after administration of ketamine versus etomidate in emergency department patients undergoing RSI. Method(s): This IRB-approved, prospective randomized study evaluated adult patients undergoing RSI from 1/1/2018 to 3/31/2018 in a 108-bed emergency department. Randomization assigned patients based on calendar day. Patients intubated on even days received etomidate IV (0.3 mg/kg) and on odd days received ketamine IV (2 mg/kg). The primary outcome was the incidence of hypotension, defined as a 20% decrease in systolic blood pressure (SBP), between pre- and post-intubation vitals. Post intubation vitals were collected until either 15 minutes after induction or until an additional sedative was given. Secondary outcomes were absolute change in SBP, absolute change in MAP, number of intubation attempts, vasopressor use, paralytic selection and time to additional sedatives. Data was analyzed using SPSS, p-value <0.05 considered significant. Result(s): This interim analysis included 68 patients (ketamine n=37, etomidate n=31); primary indications for intubation included respiratory failure (57.5%) and trauma (25%). Baseline characteristics were similar between groups. Incidence of post RSI hypotension significantly increased in the group of patients receiving ketamine (11/37, 29.7%) vs those receiving etomidate (2/31, 6.5%), p=0.02. SBP was significantly lower post RSI with ketamine (136 +/- 39 Pre vs 124 +/- 45 Post, mmHg, p=0.04) but not with etomidate (128 +/- 34 Pre vs 135 +/- 40 Post, mmHg, p=0.11). All other post-RSI hemodynamics were similar between groups (DBP, HR, MAP, RR, SI). Conclusion(s): Ketamine was associated with a statistically significant increase in the incidence of hypotension post RSI compared to etomidate. The theory that ketamine has a protective effect on hemodynamic stability during rapid sequence intubation is unlikely given these results. Based on this interim analysis, ketamine should be utilized with caution in this patient population.
