T1  - Risk of nosocomial infections with etomidate use
T3  - 47th Society of Critical Care Medicine Critical Care Congress, SCCM 2018. San Antonio, TX United States.
A1  - Amin V.
A1  - Mancl E.
A1  - Rech M.
A1  - Kutlu K.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Learning Objectives: Etomidate is widely used for sedation in rapid sequence intubation (RSI) of critically ill patients because it is hemodynamically neutral. It also causes adrenal suppression due to inhibition of cortisol synthesis. Although studies have largely demonstrated no difference in outcomes when used in septic patients, it is not clear if etomidate increases the risk of infection in patients who aren't septic. The purpose of this study is to determine the correlation between etomidate use and incidence of nosocomial infections compared to ketamine, propofol or no agent. Method(s): This was a retrospective cohort of hospitalized patients who received etomidate or no etomidate (ketamine, propofol or no agent) for RSI between July 2014 and July 2016. Patients were excluded if they were intubated due to sepsis, had a burn injury, or had an elective procedure. The primary endpoint was incidence of nosocomial infections and sepsis in patients within one to five days of RSI. Baseline characteristics were analyzed using descriptive statistics, while categorical data was analyzed with chi-square test or Fischer's exact test, and continuous data was analyzed with either the independent t-test or the Mann-Whitney U test. Result(s): A total of 434 patient charts were reviewed, of which 129 patients met the study criteria (n = 94 etomidate; n = 35 nonetomidate). Baseline demographics were well-matched between groups, with the exception of number of patients intubated in the medical intensive care unit (3.2% vs. 17.1%, p = 0.012) and higher mean SOFA score in the etomidate group (6.20 vs. 3.89, p < 0.001). The incidence of nosocomial infection was not statistically different between groups, though it was higher in the etomidate group (38.7% etomidate vs. 28.6% non-etomidate, p = 0.44). Patients in the non-etomidate group had a longer mean hospital stay (14.0 days vs. 18.1 days, p = 0.202) and ICU stay (11.0 days vs. 14.1 days, p = 0.300), while the etomidate group had higher rates of bacteremia (22.2% vs. 0.0%, p = 0.179). Conclusion(s): Though there was no significant difference in rates of nosocomial infection between groups, etomidate patients had 10% higher rate of infection. Future research is needed to establish a definitive correlation between etomidate use and increased rates of nosocomial infections.
