T1  - Association of ketamine with post-intubation hypotension at intubation in hemodynamically unstable patients
T3  - 2018 Annual Meeting of the Society for Academic Emergency Medicine, SAEM 2018. Indianapolis, IN United States.
A1  - Ishimaru T.
A1  - Goto T.
A1  - Takahashi J.
A1  - Okamoto H.
A1  - Hagiwara Y.
A1  - Watase H.
A1  - Hasegawa K.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Background: Tracheal intubation on hemodynamically unstable patients is a critical resuscitation procedure in the ED. While the use of etomidate or ketamine is recommended, etomidate is not approved in some countries including Japan. However, little is known about the relationship of ketamine use with the risk of post-intubation hypotension in the ED. In this context, we hypothesized that the use of ketamine is associated with a lower risk of post-intubation hypotension in hemodynamically unstable patients. Method(s): This is an analysis of the data from a multicenter prospective study of consecutive patients who underwent emergency airway management. By using a standardized protocol, the data were collected from 14 EDs across Japan from February 2012 through November 2016. The current analysis included adult (aged >=18years) non-cardiac-arrest patients who underwent tracheal intubation in the ED with a pre-intubation shock index of >=0.9. The primary exposure was the use of ketamine as sedative, with the use of midazolam or propofol as the reference. The primary outcome was post-intubation hypotension (systolic blood pressure of <=90 mmHg or >=20% decrease in systolic blood pressure). We constructed an unadjusted and adjusted two-level hierarchical models with binomial response using random intercepts for the EDs to account for patient clustering within the EDs. The multivariable analysis adjusted for age, sex, body mass index, primary indication, pretreatment, paralytics, and intubator's specialty. Result(s): Among 977 eligible patients, 316 (32%) patients underwent emergency intubation using ketamine as a sedative agent. Overall, the incidence of postintubation hypotension was 236 (24%). Patients who underwent intubation with ketamine had a lower risk of post-intubation hypotension compared to these who underwent intubation with non-ketamine sedatives (15% vs 29%, p<0.001). Likewise, in multivariable analysis, the ketamine group had a significantly lower risk of post-intubation hypotension (OR 0.43; 95%CI 0.28-0.64, p<0.001) compared to the non-ketamine group. Conclusion(s): In this prospective multicenter study of ED airway management, the use of ketamine was associated with a lower risk of post-intubation hypotension in hemodynamically unstable patients.
