T1  - Hemodynamic effects of ketamine versus etomidate for prehospital rapid sequence intubation
T3  - 47th Society of Critical Care Medicine Critical Care Congress, SCCM 2018. San Antonio, TX United States.
A1  - Stanke L.
A1  - Nakajima S.
A1  - Zimmerman L.H.
A1  - Collopy K.
A1  - Fales C.
A1  - Powers W.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Learning Objectives: Rapid sequence intubation (RSI) is often required in managing critically ill patients pre-hospital. Limited data exists to support ketamine as an alternative to etomidate, particularly in the pre-hospital setting. This study evaluated hemodynamic differences after administration of ketamine versus etomidate in pre-hospital RSI. Method(s): This IRB approved, retrospective study evaluated adult patients undergoing pre-hospital RSI from 12/2015 to 1/2017 within a regional emergency transport service. Hemodynamic data included blood pressure, heart rate, and shock index (SI) which was collected within 15 minutes prior and at least once 15 minutes post-administration or until additional sedative medications were given. Data was analyzed using SPSS with a p-value < 0.05 being considered significant. Result(s): Of 225 reviewed, 113 patients met inclusion criteria (ketamine n = 33, etomidate n = 80) with a median age 53.3 and 57.9 (p = 0.27) and a median GCS 7.7 and 7.0 (p = 0.47) respectively. The primary indications for intubation were respiratory failure and trauma. Incidence of hypotension (a 20% drop in systolic blood pressure from baseline) was no different in the ketamine vs etomidate groups (12% vs 15%, p = 0.77). Median SBP was 132 +/- 38 pre vs 136 +/- 40 post (p = NS) in the ketamine group and 140 +/- 33 pre vs 132 +/- 45 post, (p = NS) in the etomidate group. All other post-RSI hemodynamics were similar between groups. Other factors impacting more frequent decreases in SBP included age >=70 years (7% ketamine vs 37% etomidate, p = 0.04) and trauma patients (10% ketamine vs 60% etomidate, p = 0.009). Number of intubation attempts was the same for each group. There was no difference in vasopressor use. Conclusion(s): Ketamine may be considered an alternative induction agent for RSI in the pre-hospital setting. No difference in hemodynamic data was seen between the two groups. Further research is needed to assess ketamine in the special populations identified in this study.
