T1  - Treatment of alcohol withdrawal syndrome with benzodiazepines or phenobarbital in the medical intensive care unit, a retrospective comparison
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2018. San Diego, CA United States.
A1  - Saukkonen J.J.
A1  - Mekuria T.
A1  - Ronan M.
A1  - Connell G.
Y1  - 2018//
N2  - Rationale: Alcohol withdrawal syndrome (AWS) has been most commonly treated with benzodiazepines but use of phenobarbital has been increasing, supported by several small retrospective and prospective studies. Based on affiliate hospital experiences, and concern regarding prolonged hospitalizations with benzodiazepine treatment, we recently started using phenobarbital for treatment of AWS in our VA Medical Intensive Care Unit. Method(s): As a quality improvement project, we retrospectively reviewed medical records to compare lengths of stay (LOS), intubations, and the use of adjunct medications, 1:1 sitters, and restraints for patients treated with either benzodiazepines (BZD) or phenobarbital (PhB). Result(s): From 7/1/2015 to 7/15/2016 there were 28 patients admitted 37 times for AWS treated with BZD, and from 8/1/2016 to 10/4/2017 there were 27 patients admitted 40 times treated with PhB. The ICU LOS was 6.24 days for the BZD group, and 1.91 days for the PhB group, p<0.0001, t test. The hospital LOS for the BZD group was 12.73 days versus 5.67 days for the PhB patients, p<0.0001. During the BZD-treated AWS admissions, there were 6 intubations (16.2%) with a total of 33 days of intubation for the group, and an average duration of 5.5 days per patient. During PhB-treated admissions, there was 1 intubation prior to administration of PhB and 1 (2.5%) after PhB was given, with 4 total days of intubation for the group, and an average duration of 2 days per patient. Adjunct AWS medications were used in 30 BZD admissions (81%) and in 34 PhB admissions (85%), including propofol and/or dexmedetomidine in 26 BZD admissions (70%) and in 11 PhB admissions (27.5%). During the BZD-treated admissions, 19 (51%) utilized 1:1 sitters, while 14 (35%) in the PhB admissions used sitters. Restraints were used in 24 (65%) BZD admissions and 13 (33%) PhB admissions. Conclusion(s): Within our retrospective, non-randomized historical comparison, PhB-based treatment had a significantly shorter ICU and hospital LOS, lower intubation rate, and lower use of IV adjunct medications, 1:1 sitters, and restraints. These data suggest that a randomized prospective trial comparing BZD and PhB AWS treatment should be conducted.
