T1  - Safety and efficacy of non-anesthesiologist administration of propofol sedation (NAAPS) for esophagogastroduodenoscopy (EGD) in the intensive care unit (ICU)
T3  - Digestive Disease Week 2017, DDW 2017. Chicago, IL United States.
A1  - Patel J.
A1  - Fang J.C.
A1  - Staub J.
A1  - Taylor L.J.
A1  - Gawron A.J.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - Background: Patients with severe acute upper gastrointestinal bleeding (UGIB) are often admitted to the ICU and require endoscopy. These patients are at potentially greater risk of cardiopulmonary complications from sedation due to their co-morbidities and severity of illness. NAAPS has been endorsed by GI societies for conscious sedation for outpatient procedures, but safety has not been previously reported in the ICU setting. The objective of this study was to determine the safety and efficacy of NAAPS in patients undergoing EGD in the ICU for UGIB. Method(s): A retrospective chart review was performed of 100 consecutive EGD's performed in the ICU for UGIB from June 2015- September 2016. Exclusion criteria AGA Abstracts S-844 were patients age <18, on continuous vasopressors, or on a mechanical ventilator. The efficacy primary outcome was successful procedure completion, defined as reaching the 2nd portion of the duodenum or jejunum in gastric bypass patients. Safety outcomes were sedation related adverse events: hypoxia (O2 saturation<90%), hypotension (blood pressure (BP)< 90/60 mmHg) and bradycardia (<60 beats per minute), oxygen non-rebreather mask use, BiPAP, respiratory therapy assistance or intubation. Descriptive measures included patient demographics, ICU admission lab values, sedation drug dosages, and interventions performed to achieve hemostasis. Result(s): Patients were 61% male with a mean age of 63 (SD 13.96) and mean body mass index (BMI) of 27.43 (SD 6.99). Mean ICU admission lab values were hemoglobin 9.08 g/dL (SD 2.05), INR 1.56 (SD 0.63) with baseline oxygen requirement 2.66 L (SD 2.17). 9% of patients required intervention (Table 1). 86% of procedures were successfully completed; 14 procedures could not be completed for reasons including sub-optimal view due to heme or mass. Only 2% of procedures were aborted due to medical instability (Table 2). Although 100 procedures were reviewed, one procedure was aborted and no vital signs were recorded to be included in adverse events analysis. Of the 99 procedures analyzed for adverse events, 4% experienced oxygen desaturation <90% SpO2; the overall average documented low SpO2 was 97.8%. No patients required intubation or assistance by respiratory therapy, however 5% required BiPAP or non-rebreather masks. 20.2% of patients had hypotension. The overall mean lowest systolic BP was 105.7 (SD 18.92) and mean lowest diastolic BP was 58.78 (SD 12.06). 7% of patients developed bradycardia; the average lowest heart rate was 84.9 (SD 16.43). Conclusion(s): This data suggests that NAAPS is a safe and effective method for sedation for EGD in patients with UGIB in the ICU. Only 2% of procedures were aborted due to medical instability that could possibly be related to sedation. Further analysis of larger number of patients and comparison to other modes of endoscopic sedation are in progress. (Table Presented).
