T1  - Refractory status asthmaticus and use of inhaled sevoflurane
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2017. Washington, DC United States.
A1  - D'Souza J.M.
A1  - Pawar M.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - Introduction: Acute exacerbation of bronchial asthma can result in rapid and life threatening deterioration. In rare cases, therapy beyond conventional treatment is needed and inhaled volatile anesthetics have been used as potentially life-saving agents (Mondonedo et al). We present an interesting case of asthma exacerbation refractory to conventional therapy which required an inhaled anesthetic agent. Case Report Mr L is a 27 year old Chinese gentleman, smoker of 10 pack years, with bronchial asthma since childhood. He defaulted treatment with inhaled preventer and presented with sudden onset of shortness of breath with wheezing for a day. He was noted to be in distress on arrival and had bilateral extensive wheeze on auscultation. Arterial blood gasses (ABG) showed type 2 respiratory failure. He was immediately intubated and mechanically ventilated. Inhaled bronchodilators, intravenous steroid and magnesium sulphate were initiated. He was sedated, paralyzed and managed in the intensive care unit (ICU). Chest X-ray revealed no abnormality, routine blood investigations were normal, but ABG continued to show worsening type 2 respiratory failure with pH dropping from 7.24 to 7.18, and PaCO2 rising from 63 to 86 mmHg, despite standard therapy. His peak airway pressures were persistently elevated, ranging from 50-60 cmH2O with plateau pressures ranging 32-35 cmH2O. Ketamine infusion was initiated and theophylline was added but showed no benefit. He deteriorated further with a right pneumothorax (Fig-1), which was decompressed with an urgent tube thoracostomy. In view of refractory bronchospasm, with peak airway pressures rising to 68 cmH2O, decision was made to attempt a trial of inhaled anesthetic agent. Sevoflurane was initiated in consultation with anesthesiologist and showed rapid drop in peak pressures ranging from 30 to 36 cmH2O within a period of 4.5 hours of initiation and bronchospasm resolved. No adverse effects were noted. He was weaned off the ventilator, extubated 2 days later, had complete resolution of pneumothorax, was transferred out of ICU and is doing well. Discussion Status Asthmaticus when refractory, poses higher risks to life threatening complications. Sevoflorane has bronchodilatory properties and its use for refractory acute severe asthma exacerbations has been previously documented in a few case reports. In our patient, worsening hypercapnia and peak airway pressures with resultant pneumothorax prompted the use of inhaled sevoflurane, which showed dramatic response. This illustrates the usefulness of sevoflurane as a life saving therapeutic agent that must be recognized and considered in asthma exacerbations refractory to maximized conventional therapy. [Image Presented].
