T1  - Oesophageal soft food bolus obstruction causing airway compromise requiring emergency intubation plus Creon and Coca-Cola instillation on ITU
T3  - AAGBI GAT Annual Scientific Meeting 2017. Cardiff United Kingdom.
A1  - Wang D.
A1  - Woolley J.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - We present an unusual case of acute airway compromise resulting from food bolus. An 80-year-old female with multiple sclerosis was brought to A&E with shortness of breath following a meal. She had recently visited her GP with swallowing difficulties. On arrival, she was desaturating with stridor and respiratory distress. A palpable anterior neck lump was noted and a large mediastinal mass was visible on chest X-ray (Fig. 1). Rapid sequence induction with a size 6.0 ETT was performed in theatre using a McGrath MAC laryngoscope. Subsequent CT neck and thorax showed massive dilatation of the oesophagus from the epiglottis to the level of the diaphragmatic hiatus, containing food debris (Fig. 1). Marked compression of the carina was noted distal to the tube, presenting a continued risk of airway obstruction despite intubation. Endoscopic retrieval of the food bolus was attempted, but unsuccessful due to significant impaction. Creon dissolved in NaHCO3 was initially trialled but on literature review, a regimen of Creon and Coca-Cola instilled via nasogastric tube was commenced [1]. Repeat endoscopy 3 days later showed the food bolus had softened markedly and was now easily dislodged into the stomach. The patient was extubated the same day, and discharged soon after on a soft diet. Discussion There have been numerous instances of oesophageal soft food bolus obstruction (OSFBO) described, however, few have required urgent intubation due to acute airway compromise [2]. In ITU, controlled positive pressure ventilation (SIMV) with deep sedation (maintained via propofol/alfentanil infusion) was used to prevent spontaneous negative pressure breathing; thus reducing risk of further airway compromise beyond the endotracheal tube. No national guidelines exist regarding management of OSFBO [3] resulting in wide variation of practice reported, but endoscopy is often the first treatment of choice. In this case however, endoscopy initially failed to clear the impacted food bolus despite multiple attempts via instrumentation and trial of Creon plus NaHCO3. Subsequent treatment was guided by a study reporting favourable outcomes in a similar patient cohort following instillation of Creon 10000 mixed with 30 ml Coca- Cola four times daily via nasogastric tube over several days [1]. The recommended regimen was adopted for 3 days and resulted in successful clearance of the OSFBO without significant complication or need for surgical intervention. (Figure presented).
