T1  - Delirium and sedative requirements in a trial of helmet versus facemask noninvasive ventilation in acute hypoxic respiratory failure
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2017. Washington, DC United States.
A1  - Wolfe K.S.
A1  - Patel B.K.
A1  - Pohlman A.S.
A1  - Hall J.B.
A1  - Kress J.P.
Y1  - 2017//
N2  - Rationale: There is a high incidence of delirium in patients with acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS). The development of delirium is associated with worse short-term outcomes, such as mortality, and long-term cognitive impairment. Mechanical ventilation and treatment with sedative medications are associated with the development of delirium. We have previously shown that helmet noninvasive ventilation (NIV) in ARDS can significantly reduce intubation rates. It is unknown if the avoidance of mechanical ventilation with helmet NIV alters the incidence of delirium. Method(s): We performed a single-center randomized controlled trial in which we enrolled patients admitted to the medical ICU with ARDS requiring facemask noninvasive ventilation for at least 8 hours. Patients were randomly assigned to continue on noninvasive ventilation using the facemask interface (n=39) or switch to the helmet interface (n=44). All patients received assessments of sedation level using the Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale (RASS) as part of standard ICU monitoring and daily assessments for the presence of delirium using the Confusion Assessment Method for the ICU (CAM-ICU). All delirium assessments were performed at the time of a daily awakening from sedation. Daily and total sedative medication requirements were collected. Result(s): Patients randomized to Helmet NIV had a trend towards lower percentage of ICU days with delirium (0% vs 14.8%; p=0.085) and a significantly lower percentage of ICU days with delirium or coma than patients in the Facemask NIV group (0% vs 71.4%; p=0.0014). The difference in percentage of days with coma or delirium persisted throughout the hospitalization (0% vs 45.5%; p=0.0032). Less patients in the Helmet NIV group received propofol than patients in the Facemask NIV group (16% vs 41%; p=0.01). An equivalent percentage of patients in the Helmet and Facemask NIV groups received continuous opiate infusions, but the total dose received (morphine equivalents of mg/hour of ICU stay) was lower in the Helmet NIV group than the Facemask NIV group (0.01 vs 0.58; p=0.004). There was no difference between groups in the proportion of patients receiving or total dose of dexmedetomidine or midazolam. Conclusion(s): Delirium is a serious and frequent consequence of critical illness. Strategies to prevent the development of delirium are imperative in the treatment of critically ill patients. Our work suggests that an NIV strategy with the helmet interface is associated with a lower incidence of delirium and lower total sedative requirements when compared to facemask NIV.
