T1  - Ketamine versus etomidate for ED rapid sequence intubation
T3  - 2014 Annual Meeting of the Society for Academic Emergency Medicine, SAEM 2014. Dallas, TX United States.
A1  - Driver B.
A1  - Moore J.
A1  - Reardon R.
A1  - Steinberg L.
A1  - Antolick A.
A1  - Usher S.
A1  - Miner J.
Y1  - 2014//
N2  - Background: Ketamine and etomidate are commonly used for sedation in ED rapid sequence intubation (RSI), defined as receiving sedation and paralytics simultaneously to facilitate intubation. The relative effects of these agents on intubation success have not been well described. Objective(s): To compare mortality, intubation success, intubation complications, and short-term safety outcomes between ketamine and etomidate. Method(s): This is a preliminary report of a randomized clinical trial using exception from informed consent in an urban Level I trauma center. All non-prisoner adult patients who were going to receive RSI were randomized to receive either ketamine (2 mg/kg) or etomidate (0.3 mg/kg) IV for the sedation during RSI. The primary outcome is mortality at 30 days or discharge, whichever occurs first. Secondary outcomes include first-pass success, number of intubation attempts, number of post-intubation sedative boluses within 6 hours, post-intubation hypotension (sBP less than 90 mm Hg) within 6 hours, and postintubation hypoxemia (oxygen saturation less than 90%) within 2 hours. Intensive care physicians but not emergency physicians were blinded to the medication received. Categorical and continuous outcomes were compared by the chi-square test and Mann-Whitney U test, respectively. This trial is registered at clinicaltrials.gov: NCT01823328. Result(s): Ninety-eight eligible patients presented during the study period; 68 have been enrolled; 58 have preliminary data available. See Table 273. To date, there are no differences in the primary outcome (p=0.34) or any secondary outcome between patients who received ketamine or etomidate for RSI in the ED. Conclusion(s): We did not detect a difference between etomidate and ketamine in mortality, intubation success rate, intubation complications, ED hypotension, or short-term hypoxemia in patients undergoing RSI in the emergency department. (Table presented).
