T1  - An unusually difficult case of delirium requiring treatment with a barbituate induced coma and electroconvulsive therapy (ECT)
T3  - 2013 Annual Scientific Meeting of the American Geriatrics Society. Grapevine, TX United States.
A1  - Koliasko N.
A1  - Sturz R.
A1  - Mead V.
A1  - Hofmann M.T.
A1  - Dementovych N.
A1  - Ritter A.Z.
A1  - Delaney M.A.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Mr. C is a 72 y.o.male with a PMH of dementia and bipolar affective disorder. He was hospitalized at our academic teaching community medical center for Bell's mania with delirium. All medical causes were thoroughly worked up and found to be non-contributory by our geriatric medical team. Multiple behavioral and medical therapies were attempted, including Haloperidol, Olanzapine, Risperidone, Melatonin, Zolpidem, Lorazepam, Divalproex Sodium and Trazodone.Mr. C. continued to deteriorate and had been witnessed by hospital staff to not sleep or eat for several weeks. It was felt he was at risk of dying from dehydration and nutritional compromise caused by severe delirium. At that point the attending geriatric psychiatrist felt a trial of propofol-induced coma was indicated.There is literature to suggest this treatment can re-wire the brain and disrupt the delirium. The patient was transferred to the ICU, intubated,and the intensivist service was consulted for assistance with medical management during the propofol coma, which lasted approximately 4 days. After reversal of his coma, the patient was somewhat improved and deemed medically stable for treatment of his refractory delirium with ECT.With multiple ECT treatments, the delirium cleared and the patient was successfully discharged home. This case exemplifies patient safety and quality in a situation requiring a careful analysis of the risk benefit ratio of planned interventions. At first it may seem that a medically-induced coma in the ICU and ECT requiring anesthesia may go against the principles of geriatric medicine, where "less is more" and aggressive measures are generally thought to be less safe. However, considering the high mortality rate from Bell's mania with delirium (up to 80% of cases), a conservative approach would not have been appropriate. In the case of many elderly, frail, and demented patients a more reasonable, humane treatment could be hospice treatment with high doses of medications for symptom relief. However, as geriatric care professionals we must not lose our ability to individualize the best plan for our patients by considering pre-morbid function of the patient as well as patient and family preferences when we discuss goal-setting and potential outcomes.
