T1  - Clinical hypotension with etomidate or ketamine used for intubation in a medical intensive care unit
T3  - 43rd Critical Care Congress of the Society of Critical Care Medicine, SCCM 2014. San Francisco, CA United States.
A1  - Van Berkel M.
A1  - Exline M.
A1  - Mount K.
A1  - Ryder L.
A1  - Philips G.
A1  - Ali N.
A1  - Doepker B.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Introduction: Etomidate is a commonly used agent for intubation with minimal hemodynamic effects. It is a reversible inhibitor of 11-beta-hydroxylase, which suppresses the production of cortisol for at least 24 hours after a single dose. This effect on the adrenal system may have a significant impact on a patient's ability to maintain hemodynamic stability. Ketamine also has minimal effects on hemodynamics and no proven influence on the adrenal system. Method(s): A retrospective cohort study was performed to compare the incidence of clinical hypotension between ketamine and etomidate in a medical intensive care unit (ICU). Clinical hypotension was defined as: mean arterial pressure (MAP) decrease > 40% and MAP <70 mmHg, MAP <60 mmHg, systolic blood pressure (SBP) < 90mmHg for >15 minutes, initiation of vasopressors, or increase to > 30% of the pre-intubation vasopressor dose. Patients were evaluated for clinical hypotension and vasopressor requirements at various time intervals within the 24 hour period after receiving the medications. In addition, patients were evaluated for length of hospital and ICU stay, mechanical ventilation free days, and mortality. A subgroup analysis of patients with sepsis was performed. Data were compared using Chi Square test for categorical data and Mann-Whitney U test for continuous data. Multivariable logistic regression was used to evaluate the incidence of clinical hypotension 24 hours post intubation and vasopressor requirements. Result(s): A total of 178 patients were included for analysis (87 etomidate and 91 ketamine). No statistical difference was found in the primary outcome of clinical hypotension within 24 hours (72% ketamine vs. 73% etomidate, p=0.86) or at any pre-specified time interval. Baseline vasopressor requirements were statically higher in the ketamine group one hour prior to intubation (8% vs. 23.1%, p=0.03). A random-effects logistic regression model was applied and there was still no statistical difference in clinical hypotension at any time point post intubation. There was also no difference in ICU or hospital length of stay, or mortality in the entire patient population or in the septic population (n= 135). Conclusion(s): Administration of etomidate for intubation did not increase the incidence of clinical hypotension 24 hours post intubation when compared to ketamine in the entire study cohort or in septic patients. Large, prospective trials are warranted to confirm these results.
