T1  - Effects of increased intra-abdominal pressure on splanchnic circulation obtained by gastric air tonometry, in a non traumatic porcine model
T3  - 2012 European Society for Surgical Research, ESSR Congress. Lille France.
A1  - Correa-Martin L.
A1  - Castellanos Escrig G.
A1  - Garcia-Lindo M.
A1  - Diaz-Guemes Martin-Portugues I.
A1  - Moreno Naranjo B.
A1  - Sanchez-Margallo F.M.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Introduction: Gastrointestinal tract is one of the first organs to be affected in the presence of an inappropriate metabolism of oxygen, due to its anatomical and functional characteristics. Therefore, this system is considered the most sensitive organ to the presence of intra abdominal hypertension (IAH). Tonometry is usually used to determine the degree of ischemia in the gastrointestinal tract, because it gives accurate indication of the pH of the gastric and intestinal mucosa layers. Abdominal perfusion pressure (APP) is considered an indicator of adequate flow of the splanchnic vessels and the best prognostic indicator of survival in intensive care units. Objective(s): The main purpose of this study was to assess the prognostic value of gastric air tonometry as a predictor of inadequate splanchnic perfusion in a porcine model of nontraumatic intra abdominal hypertension, and determine its relation with APP. Materiel/Patients and Methods: The study was approved by the ethics committee of animal welfare of our centre. Our sample consisted of 15 female swine (Large White), with an average weight of 22 kg. All subjects were premedicated intramuscularly with atropine (0.04 mg/kg), diazepam (0.4 mg/kg) and ketamine (10 mg/kg), and anaesthesia was induced with propofol (3 mg/ kg/IV). Finally they were intubated with endotracheal tubes and connected to a semi-circular loop attached to a mechanical ventilator. Ventilation was controlled to normocapnia values. According to established intra-abdominal pressure (IAP), animals were divided randomly into three groups of 5 animals each: normal IAP (control group) and raised IAP up to 20 mmHg (group 20) and 30 mmHg (group 30), respectively. In the raised IAP groups, pressure was maintained for 3 hours by abdominal insufflation of CO2. Data about the acidity of the gastric mucosa was determined by measuring the intramucosal gastric pH (pHim) and PgCO2 using a gastric tonometry monitor (Datex-Ohmeda S/5tm tonometry module E-Tono by GE Healthcare), and inserting the gastric catheter into the stomach (TonometricsTM Catheter 14F). Using IAP and mean arterial pressure (MAP) values we indirectly obtained the abdominal perfusion pressure (APP) with the following formula: APP = MAP - IAP. The measure ments were registered at nine different times throughout the study. Result(s): pHimg showed a decrease in groups 20 and 30, with this difference being statistically significant in group 30 in measurements performed 90min after IAP increase. The values of PgCO2 were increased in groups 20 and 30, being statistically significant in group 30, 120 min after increased IAP. There were no statistically differences observed in any parameter regarding applied IAP. In the control group a normal APP was observed at all times. In increased IAP groups there is a time progressive decrease of this parameter, with statistically significant differences being observed between groups 30 min after increased IAP. Conclusion(s): Gastric mucous sensitivity to IAP can be studied performing a minimally invasive technique (gastric tonometry), thus assessing the main complication predictive factor, such as splanchnic hypoperfusion.
