T1  - Substantial site-level variance exists in adverse tracheal intubation associated events: Near 4 kids
T3  - 43rd Critical Care Congress of the Society of Critical Care Medicine, SCCM 2014. San Francisco, CA United States.
A1  - Nishisaki A.
A1  - Nett S.
A1  - Emeriaud G.
A1  - Jarvis D.
A1  - Montgomery V.
A1  - Nadkarni V.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Introduction: Airway management in Pediatric ICU is associated with adverse events: tracheal intubation associated events (TIAEs). Patient, provider and practice factors were associated with those events, but site-level variance and the role of site-level characteristics are unknown. We hypothesize a site-level variance exists in the incidence of TIAEs and a site characteristics (size, academic vs. community, case mix, presence of fellowship program) would affect this outcome. Method(s): TI quality improvement data were prospectively collected for initial TI in 15 PICUs in US and Canada, 7/2010-12/2011 using National Emergency Airway Registry for Children (NEAR4KIDS) tool with predetermined site-specific compliance plans to ensure >90% of TI data capture. TIAEs and severe TIAEs were explicitly defined by NEAR4KIDS operational definitions. Site-level characteristics (size, academic or community, mixed with cardiac surgery or separate, presence of fellowship program) were collected separately. The association of site-level characteristics with occurrence of TIAEs was analyzed using univariate analysis, and with a hierarchal model adjusting for previously identified patient and provider-level risk factors. Chi2 and random- effect model were used with p value<0.05 as significant. Linear correlation was used to evaluate association between the frequency of indication and medication use at site level. Result(s): Analysis of 1720 initial TIs from 15 PICUs revealed incidence of TIAEs of 20%, severe TIAEs: 6.5 %, with site variability in TIAEs (range: 0-44%), and severe TIAEs (0-20%). A larger PICU size (>26 beds) was associated with fewer TIAEs (18% vs. 23%, p=0.006), but not the presence of fellowship (20% vs.18%, p=0.58) in unadjusted analysis. When adjusting for patient (age, history of difficult airway, cardiac surgical condition, unstable hemodynamics) and provider (resident participation) characteristics, both size of PICU and fellowship presence became non-significant (p=0.44, and p=0.18, respectively). Presence of mixed ICU with cardiac surgery was independently associated with higher incidence of TIAEs even after adjusted for patient-level cardiac condition (25% vs.15%, p<0.001, adjusted OR: 1.81; 95% CI:1.29-2.53,p=0.01). Substantial site-level variance was observed in TI medication use: atropine: 7-78%, midazolam&fentanyl: 0-65%, propofol: 0-88%, ketamine: 0-67%), however, the use of atropine and the proportion of infants per site had no correlation (r=0.103, p=0.72). Similarly, the use of ketamine and shock state, the use of propofol and elective indication were not correlated at the site level (r= 0.48, p=0.07; r= -0.001, p=1.00, respectively). Indirect laryngoscopy was infrequently used as first device everywhere (1.7%). The rate of resident involvement as a first airway provider varied significantly across the centers (0-78%, average 23%). Conclusion(s): Substantial site-level variance exists in incidence of adverse TIAEs. Neither PICU size nor training structure explained this site-level variance. Substantial site-level variance was also observed in the types of TI medication use and could not be explained by common indications. Further studies should explore the source of site-level variability in practice and safety outcomes, and identify potential targets for quality improvement activity.
