T1  - Evaluation of the feasibility of thoracic surgery without general anaesthesia and tracheal intubation
T3  - 2013 Annual Meeting of the International Anesthesia Research Society, IARS 2013. San Diego, CA United States.
A1  - Elshek S.M.
A1  - Kamal J.M.
A1  - Nabil D.
A1  - Sherif A.A.
A1  - Abdelrahman A.A.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - introduction : Awake thoracotomy was implemented to improve the outcome of thoracic surgery in selected patients by using high thoracic epidural anesthesia (TEA). In this randomized controled study we assessed the feasibility and advantage of performing tubeless thoracic surgery under TEA during spontaneous respiration in conscious sedated cancer patients. methods : Thirty patients scheduled for thoracic surgery were enrolled in the study. The patients were randomly assigned to receive either sole thoracic epidural anesthesia at any level between T3-T6 with ketofol sedation (group1: tubeless group) or receive general anesthesia with one lung ventilation (OLV) and thoracic epidural analgesia (group 2: control group). Peri-operative data, surgical outcome, patient satisfaction, technical feasibility, procedure cost, and postoperative recovery were recorded. results : The study groups were matched in demographic data, technical feasibility and anesthesia satisfaction. Peri-operatively, there were no significant difference between both groups regarding arterial oxygenation represented as arterial oxygen tension to fraction of inspired oxygen (PaO2/FiO2) , arterial carbon dioxide tension (PaCO2), heart rate and mean arterial pressure. Total operating room, anesthesia, recovery time and duration of intensive care unit (ICU) stay were significantly longer in the control group. The anesthetic costs were found to be significantly reduced in tubless thoracotomy group compared to the control group. discussion : We concluded that Tubeless thoracic surgery in conscious sedated cancer patients is safely feasible, it resulted in comparable patient satisfaction with less cost, shorter global operating room time and ICU stay than the same procedures performed under general anesthesia. (Table Presented).
