T1  - Combined ketamine and propofol sedation versus propofol sedation for emergency department procedures: A prospective randomized trial
T3  - American College of Emergency Physicians, ACEP Research Forum 2013. Seattle, WA United States.
A1  - Sawas A.
A1  - Youngquist S.T.
A1  - Madsen T.E.
A1  - Davis V.W.
Y1  - 2013//
N2  - Study Objectives: To compare adverse events with procedural sedation using ketofol (1:1 ketamine/propofol mixture) vs. propofol alone. Method(s): This was a prospective, randomized, single-blind study. Subjects were randomized to receive either ketofol or propofol. Clinical respiratory depression (CRD), which included any placement of an airway, airway maneuvers, bag-valve mask ventilation or intubation. Sub-clinical respiratory depression (S-CRD), defined as the occurrence of any of the following during the sedation: an increase or decrease in ETCO2 by 10 points from baseline, an ETCO2 greater than 50 mmHg or a loss of ETCO2 for greater than 15 seconds (apnea). The target enrollment was 65 patients in each group (N=130 total). Bayesian analysis using an informed prior based on three previous studies was performed with respect to CRD. Result(s): Ninety-nine patients were enrolled (48 ketofol and 51 propofol), 55 (56%) males and 44 (44%) females. Ninety percent of the participants were ASA class 1 or 2. Mean age was 45 +/- 18 years. Procedures: 82 (83%) fracture/dislocation reductions, 7 (7%) cardioversion, 4 (4%) abscess drainage, 3 (3%) foreign body removal, 1 (1%) burn debridement and 1 (1%) chest tube placement. The rate of clinical respiratory depression (CRD) was 17/51 (33%) in the propofol group compared to 10/48 (21%) in the ketofol (P= 0.163). S-CRD was more prevalent 35/ 51 (69%) with propofol compared to 20/48 (42%) with ketofol (P=0.007). Combined CRD/S-CRD was more prevalent among the propofol group 37/51 (73%) compared to 25/48 (52%) among the ketofol group (P= 0.035). Loss of ETCO2 wave form was 28/43 (65%) in the propofol group compared to 14/34 (41%) in the ketofol group (P= 0.036). Bayesian analysis resulted in a median RR=0.82 for CRD favoring ketofol with 95% highest posterior density (HPD) limits of 0.63-1.06. The probability that the RR is less than 1, favoring ketofol, is estimated at 0.94, leaving a probability of only 0.06 that ketofol is not protective for respiratory events. The probability lying below RR=0.95 is 0.87 and below RR=0.80 is 0.77. Conclusion(s): Ketofol was associated with a lower relative risk of adverse events when compared to propofol with fairly high probability when evaluated using a Bayesian approach with informed priors based on existing studies.
