T1  - Implementation of an updated analgesia, sedation and delirium protocol in a trauma-surgical intensive care unit is associated with decreased duration of mechanical ventilation
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2012. San Francisco, CA United States.
A1  - Dale C.
A1  - Kannas D.A.
A1  - Fan V.
A1  - Hough C.L.
A1  - Treggiari M.
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - Background: Mechanical ventilation is common in the ICU and is associated with pain, delirium and agitation. Professional society guidelines have been developed to synthesize the clinical trial data and guide clinicians. Order sets and protocols are commonly used at the ICU level to implement guidelines. We hypothesized that an update of our order set would decrease the average duration of mechanical ventilation as well as the average amount of analgesic and sedative medications given. Method(s): A large public university-affiliated trauma-surgical ICU underwent an analgesia, sedation and delirium order set update process. The baseline protocol was implemented in 2004 and lacked several features of the most recent 2002 three-society analgesia, sedation and delirium guidelines. An updated, more guideline concordant order set was created. Significant changes are listed in Figure 1. We compared one year of patient data and outcomes before the implementation of the updated protocol to a one year period after implementation of the new protocol. We converted opiate doses to morphine equivalents and benzodiazepine doses to lorazepam equivalents. We built a multivariable model with a priori specified variables to explore the association of the updated order set on the duration of mechanical ventilation and the amount of analgesic and sedative medications used on a daily basis. Skewed variables were log-transformed. Result(s): 703 intubated patients were cared for in our ICU the 12 month period prior to protocol implementation and 780 were cared for in the 12 months after protocol implementation. The gender, age and modified SAPS score were similar between the two groups (Table 1). Multivariable analysis demonstrated a 19.5% reduction in the average duration of mechanical ventilation (95% CI 7.1% to 30.3%, p < 0.05) (Table 2, Figure 2). We also saw a 13.4% reduction in average daily opiate usage (95% CI 2.1% to 23.3%, p < 0.05); a 48.6% reduction in average daily benzodiazepine usage (95% CI 38.6% to 57.0%, p < 0.05); and a 41.8% increase in the amount of average daily propofol usage (95% CI 16.3% to 72.8%, p < 0.05). Conclusion(s): Implementation of an updated, more guideline-concordant sedation, analgesia and delirium order set was associated with decreased duration of mechanical ventilation as well as decreased daily opiate and benzodiazepine usage. Average daily propofol usage increased. (Table Presented).
