T1  - Effect of sedation medication and daily awakening on delirium in the mechanically ventilated critically ill patient: A descriptive pilot study
T3  - 42nd Critical Care Congress of the Society of Critical Care Medicine, SCCM 2013. San Juan Puerto Rico.
A1  - Perry T.
A1  - Moon J.
A1  - Sikand H.
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - Introduction: Although daily awakenings are recommended in mechanically ventilated patients, evidence of delirium reduction is lacking. This prospective, randomized, open-label, pilot study will determine if there are differences in delirium, days ventilated, and ICU length of stay between lorazepam, propofol, dexmedetomidine, and fentanyl treatment arms in the setting of scheduled daily awakenings. Hypothesis: We hypothesize that implementation of daily awakenings will reduce the overall incidence of delirium, as well as negate differences in delirium incidence, days ventilated, and days in the ICU. Method(s): Adults admitted to the medical ICU were enrolled if mechanically ventilated. Patients were excluded for severe neurological deficits, alcohol dependency, chronic benzodiazepine use, need for neuromuscular blockade, presence of acute myocardial infarction, heart rate less than 50 beats per minute, mean arterial pressure less than 65mmHg, or inability to be randomized within 48 hours of intubation. The primary outcomes were the incidence of delirium with daily awakenings and between the four treatment arms in the setting of daily awakenings. Secondary outcomes included days ventilated and ICU length of stay. Result(s): Baseline characteristics were not significantly different between the four treatment arms. There was significantly less delirium (-40.5 percent, P-value 0.001), and significantly fewer days in the ICU (-3.11 days, P-value 0.001) with daily awakenings compared to a historical control without daily awakenings. The incidence of delirium was significantly higher with propofol compared to dexmedetomidine (78.5 percent versus 5 percent, P-value 0.025). Time ventilated was not significantly different between the four treatment arms (P-value 0.685); nor was the ICU length of stay (P-value 0.646). Conclusion(s): Scheduled daily awakening decreased the incidence of delirium and ICU length of say compared to historical data without daily awakenings. Dexmedetomidine demonstrated a lower incidence of delirium compared to propofol. The average times spent mechanically ventilated and in the ICU were equivalent between lorazepam, propofol, dexmedetomidine, and fentanyl in the setting of daily awakenings.
