T1  - Anesthesia induction improves sublingual microcirculation
T3  - 65th Annual Meeting of the Canadian Cardiovascular Society. Toronto, ON Canada.
A1  - Drullinsky D.
A1  - Noiseux N.
A1  - Bracco D.
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - BACKGROUND: Microcirculation disturbances are hypothesized to be the motor of shock and organ dysfunction. Aggressive shock resuscitation is associated with improvement of micro-circulation in shock states. Most investigation in critically ill patients are done while administering hypnotic and analgesic drugs. The purpose of the present investigation was to investigate the effect of anesthesia drugs on microcirculation in non shocked patients. METHOD(S): After written consent, the sublingual micro-circulation of 73 patients was measured before and 20' after anesthesia induction using side-stream dark-field imaging. All patients received 1.5-3 mg/kg propofol, 2-3 M-g/kg fentanyl and 0.6 mg rocuro-nium for induction and endotracheal intubation. Patients were randomly assigned to intravenous anesthesia (n=22), sevoflurane (n=25) or desflurane (n=26) to achieve a BIS of 40-60. Microcirculation was analyzed blindly and functional capillary density and flow was categorized in a semi-quantitative manner. RESULT(S): Anesthesia induction was associated with a decrease in mean arterial pressure and an significant improvement in microcirculation (Table). There was no increase in total capillary density but there was a decrease in poorly perfused vessels and an increase in adequately perfused vessels. There was an increase in small perfused capillaries (4-10 jam) and a decrease in larger vessels (Figure). There were no difference between the three anesthesia drugs. CONCLUSION(S): Improvement in microcirculation are associated with anesthesia drug administration. Further studies are required to understand the mechanism of this microcirculation opening. This factor has to be taken into account when measuring the microcirculation in sedated ICU patients. (Table Presented) (Graph Presented).
