T1  - Risk factors, management and outcomes of near fatal asthma: A five year retrospective cohort study at an intensive care unit in Saudi Arabia
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2012. San Francisco, CA United States.
A1  - Al-Dorzi H.M.
A1  - Al-Shammary H.
A1  - Al-Shareef S.
A1  - Shammout K.
A1  - Tlayjeh H.
A1  - Arabi Y.M.
Y1  - 2012//
N2  - Rationale: Near fatal asthma (NFA) is a life threatening form of asthma. It has not been well studied in Saudi Arabia, which is unique in its weather, culture and healthcare system, all of which might affect asthma control. We studied NFA risk factors in NFA patients admitted to a tertiary care intensive care unit (ICU) in Riyadh and described their management and outcomes. Method(s): This was a retrospective cohort study of all NFA patients (>14 years) admitted between 01/01/2006 and 31/12/2010 to a 21-bed ICU (~1000 admissions/year) of a 900-bed hospital in Riyadh. NFA cases were first identified from an electronic ICU database followed by medical record review and were defined as presentation with severe respiratory distress, PCO2>45 mm Hg and requirement for invasive mechanical ventilation (MV) due to asthma. To evaluate NFA risk factors, randomly selected patients admitted to medical ward within the same period because of asthma exacerbation per medical records coding were used as controls. Collected data included: demographics, smoking history, home use of bronchodilators and inhaled corticosteroids, asthma-related emergency department (ED) visits in the preceding year, previous intubation and ICU admission, admission laboratory findings, various treatments including ventilator-care and medications, MV duration, clinical diagnosis of critical illness neuromyopathy, requirement for tracheotomy and hospital mortality. Analysis was done using SPSS software. Result(s): Thirty NFA cases were admitted to the ICU in the 5-year period and were compared to 120 controls. NFA patients were younger ( 37.5+/-19.9 years vs. 50.3+/-23.1 years, p=0.004), more males (70.0% vs. 41.7%, p=0.005), had lower prior use of inhaled steroid/long acting beta agonist combination(13.6% vs. 38.7% p=0.024), lower admission pH (7.06+/-0.13 vs. 7.36+/-0.08, p<0.001) but had similar ED visit numbers in the preceding year (3.5+/-6.1 vs. 1.6+/-2.0, p=0.12) and previous intubation (8.7% vs. 7.8%, p=0.89). Most (76.7%) NFA cases presented in cool months (October-March) compared to 57.4% for controls, p=0.054. On multivariate analysis, age (odds ratio [OR] 0.96, 95% confidence interval [CI] 0.92-0.99, p=0.015) and number of ER visits in preceding year (OR 1.20, 95% CI 0.99-1.45, p=0.07) were associated with NFA. NFA management included zero PEEP (50.0%), intravenous methylprednisolone (96.6%), ketamine infusion (56.7%) and aminophylline infusion (19.2%). NFA patients had the following outcomes: neuromyopathy (20%), MV duration=6.4+/-4.7 days, tracheostomy (13.3%) and no mortality. Conclusion(s): In our study, NFA was uncommon and associated with younger age and higher number of ED visits in the preceding year. It had significant morbidity but very low mortality.
