T1  - Combined ketamine and propofol sedation vs propofol sedation for emergency department procedures: A prospective randomized trial
T3  - 2011 Annual Meeting of the Society for Academic Emergency Medicine, SAEM. Boston, MA United States.
A1  - Sawas A.
A1  - Davis V.
A1  - Youngquist S.
A1  - Ahern M.
A1  - Johnson D.
A1  - Wilson C.
A1  - Enslow M.
A1  - Madsen T.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Background: Ketofol is suggested as an ideal agent for procedural sedation. Its relative safety and efficacy compared to propo- fol is unclear. (Figure Presented) Objectives: To compare adverse events, procedural success, and satisfaction with procedural sedation using ketofol (1:1 keta- mine/propofol mixture) vs. propofol alone. Method(s): This was a prospective, randomized, single-blind study. Subjects were randomized to receive either ketofol or pro- pofol according to a standardized protocol. Adverse events, including hypotension, placement of an airway, airway maneuvers, bag- valve mask breaths, intubation, vomiting, aspiration, 10-point rise or drop in end tidal CO2 (ETCO2), loss of ETCO2 waveform, or ETCO2 >50 were recorded. Following sedation, questionnaires were completed by the sedating physician, consultant physician, nurse, and patient. ETCO2 wave forms were also reviewed. The target enrollment was 65 patients in each group (n=130 total). These are results of a planned, Bayesian interim analysis using a flat prior and Markov-Chain Monte Carlo techniques. Result(s): Thirty-two patients were enrolled with 8 males and 8 females in each treatment group. Mean age was 43.97 +/- 16.55 years. Procedures: 25 fracture/dislocation reductions, five abscess drainage, one cardioversion, and one ear foreign body removal. The procedure was successful in 13/16 (81%) in each group. At least one pre-specified adverse event occurred in 4/16 (25%) patients in the ketofol group and in 8/16 (50%) in the propofol group. The median posterior relative risk (RR) for any adverse event for ketofol was 0.46 with 95% credible interval of 0.14-1.17 (see the Figure). Cumulative posterior probabilities that the RR for this association is variously <1.0, <0.95, or <0.90 were 0.948, 0.935, and 0.918, respectively. Provider satisfaction with both ketofol and propofol was high: median 9/10 (IQR 8-10) and 9/10 (IQR 7-9), respectively. Similar ratings were reported by consultants: median 10/10 (IQR 8-10) for each. 14/16 ketofol and 15/16 propofol patients reported willingness to undergo such sedation again. Conclusion(s): Ketofol was associated with a lower relative risk of adverse events when compared to propofol with fairly high probability when evaluated using a Bayesian approach in an interim analysis. As the 95% credible interval includes 1, the decision was made to continue enrollment until the next planned analysis. Satisfaction is high with both regimens.
