T1  - Dexmedetomidine as a sedative adjuvant to anaesthetics in off-pump coronary bypass surgery in hypertensive patients
T3  - 26th Annual Meeting of the European Association of Cardiothoracic Anaesthesiologists, EACTA 2011. Vienna Austria.
A1  - Tewari P.
A1  - Goyal P.
A1  - Pande S.
A1  - Agarwal S.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Introduction. Dexmedetomidine (Dex) is a potent alpha 2 agonist with sedative and analgesic properties. Patients with hypertension show more intraoperative haemodynamic lability with subsequent use of many drugs and anaesthetics. We used Dex in hypertensive patients undergoing off-pump coronary bypass (OPCAB) surgery in a randomized, prospective and observational trial. Method. After institutional approval and written informed consent 40 consecutive, known hypertensive patients for OPCAB surgery were randomized to receive Dex (Group A) or no Dex (Group B). The monitoring techniques, induction and maintenance of anaesthesia, surgeon, and the surgical technique were similar in both the groups. In Gr A, Dex was used as bolus 0.7 microgm/kg at the time of induction and followed by 0.2 mi-crogm kg-1 h-1 infusion throughout the surgery and up to 12 h postoperatively. BIS level of 40 was used as the target for surgical anaesthesia. Data was collected at time points and they were: 1) after intubation, 2) after IMA dissection, 3) after LIMA to LAD graft, 4) after RSVG to PDA, 5) after RSVG to circumflex arteries, 6) at sternal closure, 7) at 6 h and 8) at 12 h in the postoperative period. The observed data included heart rate, blood pressure, cardiac output, SVR, SVV, BIS value, urine output and glucose and lactate levels. Total dose of IV fluids, propofol, norepinephrine and SNP were recorded. In ICU, total dose of morphine, ventilation time, total dose of vasoconstrictors and ionotropes were recorded. SPSS ver. 10 was used for analysis. ANOVA, chi-squared and Student's t-test were used as appropriate. P<0.05 was significant. Results. One patient in Gr B was converted to an on-pump procedure, therefore 20 patients in Gr A and 19 in Gr B were analysed. The groups were similar in demography. Significant changes are shown in the table: (Table presented) Discussion. Use of Dex preserved stable haemodynamics throughout. There was reduction in total dose of propo-fol, SNP and morphine. Use of vasoconstrictors and fluids were similar in both groups. The ventilation time was less in Gr A. Troponin I, though was seen to rise in 3 patients in ICU in the non Dex group as compared to 1 in the Dex group but it was not significant. Dex seems to be a good adjunct to anaesthetics in hypertensive patients undergoing OPCAB surgery.
