T1  - Etomidate and mortality in cirrhotic patients with septic shock
T3  - American Thoracic Society International Conference, ATS 2011. Denver, CO United States.
A1  - Arabi Y.
A1  - Cherfan A.
A1  - Tamim H.
A1  - Al-Jumah A.
A1  - Rishu A.
A1  - Al-Abdulkareem A.
A1  - Al knawy B.
A1  - Hajeer A.
A1  - Tamimi W.
A1  - Brits R.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Rationale: Etomidate use in the critically ill population is controversial as it is associated with increased incidence of adrenal insufficiency with debated effects on mortality. Considering that adrenal insufficiency is a well documented entity in liver cirrhotic patients; we thought to evaluate the association of a single dose effect of etomidate given prior to intubation on the mortality of septic cirrhotic patients and the impact of the subsequent use of low dose hydrocortisone supplementation. Method(s): Our study is a nested cohort study within a randomized double blind placebo controlled study; carried at a tertiary care medical center intensive care unit (ICU); evaluating the use of low dose hydrocortisone in cirrhotic septic patients. We identified 62 intubated patients out of the 75 patients randomized to the initial study. Patients who received etomidate prior to intubation (Etomidate Group) were compared to those that did not receive etomidate (Control Group) for 28 day all cause mortality as a primary outcome. Secondary outcomes included ICU and hospital mortality, ICU length of stay, shock reversal, ventilation days and vasopressor doses. Result(s): Twenty three of the 62 intubated patients received an etomidate dose prior to intubation. Etomidate use was not associated with all cause 28-day mortality or hospital mortality but was significantly associated with higher ICU mortality (91% vs. 64% for etomidate and controls groups, respectively; p=0.02). There was no difference between the two groups with regards to ICU length of stay, shock reversal, vasopressor requirements and ventilation days. Etomidate patients who received subsequent doses of hydrocortisone required lower doses of vasopressors and had more vasopressor-free days but no improvement in mortality. Conclusion(s): In this group of septic cirrhotic patients with very high mortality, etomidate increased ICU mortality. Subsequent use of hydrocortisone appears to have no benefit beyond decreasing vasopressor requirements. The lowest mortality was observed in patients who did not receive etomidate but received hydrocortisone.
