T1  - Analysis of sedation in the ICU by drug and Time to Extubation. Post-Hoc analysis of a prospective trial
T3  - 39th Annual Meeting of the Society for Neuroscience in Anesthesiology and Critical Care. Chicago, IL United States.
A1  - Quinones Q.J.
A1  - McDonagh D.L.
A1  - Olson D.M.
Y1  - 2011//
N2  - Introduction: Numerous choices exist for the sedation of mechanically ventilated critically ill patients. Although pharmacologic differences between drugs are known, the clinical impact of using 1 medication versus another remains unclear. To help address this question we examined the number of ventilator free days in patients sedated with midazolam infusion only, versus patients sedated with propofol only, versus a combination of sedative infusions including propofol, midazolam, and/or dexmedetomidine. Method(s): With IRB approval, patients were prospectively enrolled to examine monitoring in the ICU. Clinicians were free to choose method of sedation for mechanically ventilated patients. Data was gathered from 3 ICUs across 2 facilities within the Duke Health System. In a post-hoc analysis, patients were separated into 3 groups: those receiving midazolam only as a sedative hypnotic infusion, those receiving propofol only, and those receiving a combination of midazolam, propofol, and/or dexmedetomidine during mechanical ventilation. Ventilator free days (VFDs) were calculated as days the patient was extubated and spontaneously breathing subsequent to first intubation. VFDs were calculated up to a maximum of 30. Patients who died while being mechanically ventilated received zero VFDs. Statistical analysis was performed using single factor ANOVA and post-hoc Bonferroni corrected pair-wise comparison. Result(s): The total number of patients studied was 194. The midazolam only group contained 68, the propofol only group contained 54, the mixed sedative group contained 72. Mortality in the midazolam only group was 39.7%, 18.5% in the propofol only group, and 20.8% in the mixed sedative group. The mean ventilator free days was 13.93 in the midazolam only group, 21.86 in the propofol only group, and 17.21 in the mixed sedative group. A 1-way between groups ANOVA was conducted to compare the effect of sedation type on VFDs in midazolam only, propofol only, and mixed sedative groups. There was significant effect of sedation type on VFDs at the P=0.0012 level for the 3 groups [F(2, 191)=6.95, P=0.0012]. Post-hoc Bonferroni corrected pairwise comparison showed that the propofol infusion only group had more VFDs than the midazolam infusion only group P=0.0011. Conclusion(s): Our analysis of sedative infusion type in mechanically ventilated critically ill patients demonstrated a difference in VFDs in patient sedated with midazolam only, propofol only, or a combination of midazolam, propofol, or dexmedetomidine infusions. Post-hoc Bonferroni corrected pairwise comparison demonstrated that a statistically significant difference exists between the propofol only infusion group and the midazolam only infusion group, although trends exist between all 3 groups. We believe that this correlation demonstrates the need for further study as numerous factors are at play. While the sedative infusion chosen likely plays a role in time to extubation it is also likely that the clinician's choice of sedative infusion demonstrates an expectation of time to extubation. Prospective randomization will be necessary to differentiate the impact of drug choice on outcome from that of other clinical factors influencing drug choice.
