T1  - Influence of propofol sedation in the benefits of EPA+GLA nutrition for the treatment of severe sepsis
T3  - 39th Critical Care Congress of the Society of Critical Care Medicine's. Miami Beach, FL United States.
A1  - Arruda A.P.
A1  - Martins L.
A1  - Lima S.
Y1  - 2009//
N2  - Introduction: Enteral nutrition with an EPA+GLA+Antioxidants enteral diet is considered an important adjuvant therapy in the treatment of critically ill patients with acute lung inflammatory diseases such as ALI and ARDS. A recent meta-analysis of the available evidence associated the use of this diet with lower 28 days all-cause mortality, improvement in oxygenation status (PaO2/FiO2 ratio or P/F ratio), more ventilator-free days, more ICU-free days, and less development of new organ failures [1]. For these reasons international guidelines [2] are now recommending the enteral use of EPA+GLA for the treatment of ALI/ARDS. Hypothesis: Most of the mechanically ventilated patients receive drugs for sedation purposes and propofol represents one of the most extensively used sedatives in the ICU all over the world. Propofol (Diprivan, AstraZeneca)is a lipid-soluble drug diluted in an emulsion with elevated amounts of omega-6, containing 0.1g/ml of soybean oil and when used together with enteral nutrition the total calories offered by the enteral route need to be adjusted to compensate the calories offered parenterally and therefore less EPA+GLA is offered to the patient. This trial investigated the impact of propofol sedation in the treatment of mechanically ventilated patients with severe sepsis receiving EPA+GLA. Method(s): 60 mechanically ventilated patients with severe sepsis with a P/F ratio <300 were prospectively randomized to receive enteral nutrition with EPA+GLA (Oxepa, Abbott Nutrition) and concomitant sedation with midazolan (Dormonid, Roche) (n=30) or Propofol (n-30). Sedation was started due to intubation and adjusted to maintain a level of 3-4 (Ramsay Scale) until necessary and using a daily interruption protocol. Same procedures in terms of antibiotics and corticoids among other variables were adopted to treat both groups. The primary outcome was improvement in the P/F ratio. Ventilator-free and ICUfree days were secondary outcomes. Result(s): The baseline demographics of both groups were similar. The group sedated with propofol received a mean of 39.6 g/day of soybean oil (mean daily dose of propofol of 2.6mg/Kg/h) corresponding to 356 Kcal/day. For this reason the amount of EPA+GLA decreased significantly (EPA= 5.4 vs. 3.7; GLA= 4.8 v. 3.6; n3= 11.1 vs. 8.2, all values are g/d, all p<0.05). No changes in the P/F ratio was observed in the population sedated with propofol but statistically significant improvements were observed in the population sedated with midazolan at day 4 and day 7. The group that received sedation with propofol also presented less MV-free days (12.4 vs/ 7.2 days, p<0.05) and less ICU-free days (9.4vs.6.8, p<0.05). Conclusion(s): The necessary adjustments in the total amount of calories delivered enterally in critically ill mechanically ventilated patients sedated with propofol leads to a potentially important drop in the provision of active pharmaconutrients (EPA+GLA) producing important changes in the benefits associated to this diet. Therefore, alternative sedative agents, preferably non-lipid soluble should be considered to reduce the impact over the nutrition therapy of these patients. [1] Pontes-Arruda et al. JPEN 32:596, 2008; [2] McClave et al. JPEN 33:277, 2009.
