T1  - Comparison of two doses of rocuronium during rapid sequence induction in the emergency department
T3  - Comparaison de deux doses de rocuronium dans l'induction anesthesique a sequence rapide au service d'urgence
A1  - Lucas P.
A1  - Lambert M.
A1  - Vergnion M.
A1  - Degesves S.
A1  - Garcet L.
Y1  - 2004//
N2  - Study goal: To evaluate and compare two different doses of rocuronium used with etomidate for rapid sequence induction of anesthesia in an emergency department. Patients and Methods: Thirty patients were included in a prospective study. They were randomly assigned to one of two groups. After induction of anesthesia with 0.2 mg/kg etomidate (at time T0), rocuronium was injected at a dose of 0.6 mg/kg (group 1) or 0.9 mg/kg (group 2). An initial attempt at intubation by an experienced emergency, physician took place 45 seconds later (T1). If this failed, a second attempt was made one minute after T0 (T2). Intubation conditions were evaluated using the Krieg scale. Intubation scores were compared using the Mann-Whitney test; time intervals were compared using Fisher's test. Result(s): The intubation scores were comparable in the two groups. Conversely, the number of successful intubations at T1 was significantly higher in group 2 (14/14-versus 9/13 in group 1). Conclusion(s): The muscular relaxation produced by the administration of rocuronium at a dose of 0.6 or 0.9 mg/kg allows endotracheal intubation within one minute in all patients. At the higher dose, all patients were intubated at 45 seconds. When succinylcholine is contraindicated, rocuronium can be used effectively for rapid sequence induction of anesthesia at a dose of 0.9 mg/kg.
