T1  - Clinical and laboratory assessment of protective ventilation versus conventional ventilation in patients undergoing coronary artery bypass surgery
A1  - Moussa S.F.
A1  - El Khlousy M.
Y1  - 2003//
N2  - Background: This study aimed at investigating the influence of protective ventilation on postoperative recovery, cytokine levels and gas exchange in patients undergoing elective coronary artery bypass surgery. Method(s): Twenty adult patients undergoing elective coronary artery bypass procedure were included in the study. These patients were divided into two equal groups. Group CV received conventional mechanical ventilation using respiratory rate (RR) 8 breath/min, tidal volume (TV) 12 ml/kg, fraction of inspired oxygen (FIO2) 1.0, and positive end-expiratory pressure (PEEP) 5 cm H2O. Group PV received protective mechanical ventilation using RR 16 breath/min, TV 6 ml/kg, FIO2 1.0, and PEEP +5 cm H2O. Perioperative anaesthetic and surgical management were standardized. Blood cytokines levels (tumour necrosis factor alpha [TNFalpha] and interleukin-8 [IL-8]) were determined twice perioperatively: 15 min after intubation and 15 min after arrival in intensive care unite (ICU). Blood gas parameters were also recorded at the same times. Time to tracheal extubation, ICU length of stay (LOS), overall postoperative LOS and frequency of postoperative pulmonary complications were recorded in the first 5 postoperative days. Result(s): Cytokine levels showed significant postoperative increase (P = 0.001 and P = 0.0001 for TNFalpha and IL-8, respectively) in both groups. Arterial oxygen tension/inspired oxygen concentration (PaO2/FIO2) ratio significantly decreased postoperatively in group CV (P = 0.024) only. Significant postoperative increase in arterial PCO2 was seen in both groups (P < 0.05). The duration of intubation and the overall postoperative LOS were significantly longer in group CV. Lower rate of respiratory tract infection and respiratory complications (atelectasis, collapse and barotrauma) was observed in group PV. Conclusion(s): The use of protective ventilation in patients undergoing elective coronary artery bypass surgery attenuates postoperative decreases in PaO2/FIO2 ratio, and improves postoperative recovery through decreasing time of intubation, postoperative pulmonary complications, and the overall postoperative LOS.
