A1  - Doi M.
A1  - Mori K.
A1  - Murakawa M.
A1  - Yoshiya I.
A1  - Taenaka N.
Y1  - 1996//
N2  - A Phase II clinical trial was performed to investigate the efficacy and safety as well as the optimal infusion rate of propofol, as a sedative in ICU patients. Propofol was infused continuously at a rate of 0.3 mg/kg/hr for the first five minutes in postoperative 96 patients who required intubation and sedation in ICU. The following infusion rate was adjusted to achieve the target level of sedation, Ramsay Score level 3 'responds to commands only', at the minimum effective dose. The result showed that propofol is an effective drug for sedation as seen in the efficacy (98.9%) and safety rate (90.6%). Moreover, the time until awakening after the termination of infusion was 53 minutes, demonstrating the rapid recovery from sedation with propofol. Of 96 patients nine (9.4%) experienced adverse reactions including six cases of hypotension, which was the most frequent, phlebitis and flush. All of which resolved or remitted during or after administration. The optimal infusion rate obtained in this study was 1.22 +/- 0.62 mg/kg/hr. Based on the distribution of the optimal infusion rates, approximately 0.3-3.0 mg/kg/hr was considered appropriate for sedation as an optimal infusion rate.
