T1  - Phase III clinical trial of propofol (ICI 35, 868) as sedative agent in the intensive care unit - Propofol v.s. diazepam
A1  - Taenaka N.
A1  - Yoshiya I.
A1  - Nishimura S.
A1  - Numata K.
A1  - Hashimoto Y.
A1  - Matsukawa S.
A1  - Horinouchi T.
A1  - Kubota T.
A1  - Ohtake K.
A1  - Amaha K.
A1  - Tsunoda Y.
A1  - Nagura T.
A1  - Okumura F.
A1  - Usuda Y.
A1  - Ikeda K.
A1  - Doi M.
A1  - Mori K.
A1  - Murakawa M.
A1  - Morio M.
Y1  - 1996//
N2  - We administered propofol as a sedative to ICU patients requiring intubation and studied its clinical usefulness. Diazepam was used as a comparator. A total of 56 patients were treated, of which 25 were given propofol (P group) and 31 diazepam (D group). Propofol achieved an efficacy of 100%, which was significantly better than the efficacy of diazepam at 74.2% (p = 0.006). There was no significant difference in safety rate between the two groups with propofol at 96.0% and diazepam at 96.8%. The incidence of adverse reactions was 4.0% in the P group (hypotension) and 3.2% in the D group (pain on injection). Both reactions disappeared and the patients recovered during sedation. Usefulness was assessed comprehensively in consideration of efficacy, safety and controllability of sedation. Propofol had a usefulness rate of 100%, which differed significantly from that of diazepam (77.4%) (p = 0.000). The time from completion of administration to extubation was 76.3 +/- 107.1 minutes in the P group and 428.8 +/- 535.7 minutes in the D group. The time to the patients' response of birth date was 86.2 +/- 121.7 minutes in the P group and 443.6 +/- 542.3 minutes in the D group. Both durations were significantly shorter in the P group than in the D group (p = 0.007, p = 0.007, respectively). These results suggest that propofol is a useful sedative for ICU patients requiring intubation.
