T1  - Multicentric survey on recognition evaluation and treatment of pain in pediatric intensive care units
T3  - ENQUETE MULTICENTRIQUE SUR LA PERCEPTION, LA RECONNAISSANCE, L'EVALUATION, LE TRAITEMENT DE LA DOULEUR EN REANIMATION PEDIATRIQUE
A1  - Annequin D.
A1  - Canoui P.
A1  - Comar L.
A1  - Blonde C.
A1  - Sicsic C.
A1  - Cloup M.
A1  - Godberg P.
A1  - Charpak Y.
Y1  - 1992//
N2  - To study variations of management of children pain in French intensive care units, 24 pediatric and neonatal intensive care units have been investigated: 7 centers (264 responses) for the feasibility study and 14 centers (randomly assigned) for the survey itself (304 responses). The data have been collected anonymously with a personal questionnaire proposed to each member of each intensive care unit. It has been asked, to rate from 1 to 10 the pain intensity attributed to 11 usual procedures. Professional status modifies significantly pain rating: physicians under rate constantly each procedures. When compared to physician's ratings, the nurse's rating are 33 p. cent superior for L.P. and tracheal suction and 22 p. cent superior for tracheal intubation and venous cannulation. Child age group modifies significantly pain rating. Differences in pain rating appear significantly between units. Three clinical cases have been proposed and the pain treatment for such cases have been asked. Great heterogeneity appears in treatment proposed for the same clinical case: from no treatment to systematic opioid administration. In the same way great variations appear in drug dosage. 322 respondents (62 p. cent) have expressed unsatisfaction regarding pain control in their unit. In open end questions nurses complained about the poor concern from medical group for pain control. Medical staffs need to be more aware of pain treatment. Lack of knowledge and difficulties to obtain disposable and current information is one of the main factor slowing the extent of pain control programs in children intensive care units.
